talk(191,'1970-10-12','I. M. Gelfand','P000302','Rota\'s finite-field Ramsey conjecture',r'The dated lower block formulates a Ramsey assertion for subspaces over a fixed finite field, notes the known line case, and translates that case into projective language. The number of colors is suppressed in the notebook; it must be fixed before the ambient dimension is chosen. This is the vector-space Ramsey conjecture, not Rota\'s matroid-basis conjecture.')
item(191,'P000302','conjecture','Monochromatic subspaces over a finite field',r'The notes fix \(F_q\), integers \(\ell<k\), and assert existence of \(N\) such that a partition of the \(\ell\)-subspaces of \(F_q^N\) contains a \(k\)-subspace whose \(\ell\)-subspaces lie in one class.',r'For every prime power \(q\), every integers \(r\geq1\) and \(1\leq\ell<k\), there is \(N=N(q,r,\ell,k)\) such that every map \(c:\operatorname{Gr}_{\ell}(F_q^N)\to\{1,\ldots,r\}\) is constant on \(\operatorname{Gr}_{\ell}(E)\) for some \(k\)-dimensional \(E\leq F_q^N\).',r'Established by \citet[abstract and pp.~119--120]{grahamleebrothschild1972ramsey}; the announcement appeared in January 1972. The current search did not establish an earlier public circulation date.',refs=['grahamleebrothschild1972ramsey'],cls='B',reconstruction=r'The explicit finite color parameter \(r\), and its position before \(N\), are supplied. Without that order of quantifiers, coloring every subspace differently disproves the literal reading.',problem='GP-1970-20',status='Solved; the finite-field, fixed-finite-color formulation is essential.',verification='publisher metadata checked')
item(191,'P000302','remark','The line case and its projective interpretation',r'\ru{Для \(\ell=1\) доказана.} A projective line is sought in one of two classes of projective points.',r'For fixed \(q,r,k\), sufficiently large projective space \(\mathbf P^{N-1}(F_q)\), colored with \(r\) colors, contains a monochromatic projective \((k-1)\)-space. Vector-space dimension is one greater than projective dimension.',r'This is the \(\ell=1\) specialization of \citet[abstract]{grahamleebrothschild1972ramsey}. The notebook itself attests that this specialization was already known to the seminar; this run did not identify the exact earlier announcement used by Gelfand.',refs=['grahamleebrothschild1972ramsey'],cls='E',match='partial',verification='indirect',confidence='medium',problem='GP-1970-20',status='Established special case; the archival assertion about prior proof is preserved without assigning an unverified pre-1970 reference.')
problem('GP-1970-20','Rota\'s finite-field vector-space Ramsey conjecture',r'For fixed \(q,r,\ell,k\), \(1\leq\ell<k\), does every \(r\)-coloring of the \(\ell\)-subspaces of a sufficiently large \(F_q^N\) admit a \(k\)-subspace monochromatic in dimension \(\ell\)?',['1970-191.1','1970-191.2'],r'Solved by the Graham--Leeb--Rothschild theorem; January 1972 announcement. No identical earlier problem was found in the supplied previous index.',['grahamleebrothschild1972ramsey'])
corr('P000302','Rota conjecture, order of quantifiers',r'\(\ell,k\) are fixed, then \(N\), with no bound on the number of partition classes.',r'Fix the finite number \(r\) of colors before choosing \(N\).',r'The scan and Russian agree in omitting \(r\). A coloring with one color per \(\ell\)-space is a counterexample to unrestricted colors. The verified 1972 announcement concerns Ramsey partitions into fixed finitely many classes.')

talk(192,'1970-10-12','I. M. Gelfand','P000304-P000305','Thompson quadratic pairs and Conway\'s lattice group',r'Gelfand reports Thompson\'s quadratic-pair classification. Kirillov then relays Thompson\'s account of Conway\'s sporadic group through a 24-dimensional lattice. The passage needs two distinctions: groups of Lie type are broader than classical groups, and the full automorphism group of the Leech lattice is a central double cover of the simple group. The reading list below this discussion is not a mathematical theorem.')
item(192,'P000304','definition','Quadratic pairs',r'\((G,M)\): \(M\) is a faithful irreducible \(F_p\)-representation and \(G\) is generated by elements satisfying \(M(g-1)^2=0\).',r'A quadratic pair consists of a nontrivial finite group \(G\), an odd prime \(p\), and a faithful irreducible finite-dimensional \(F_pG\)-module \(M\) such that \(G=\langle Q\rangle\), where \(Q=\{g\ne1:(g-I)^2M=0\}\). Such generators have Jordan blocks of sizes at most two, with at least one size-two block.',r'Thompson\'s Nice ICM lecture circulated in 1970 and appeared in 1971; see \citet[p.~375, Definition]{thompson1971quadratic}. The later treatment is \citet[Hypothesis~1.0]{chermak2004quadratic}.',refs=['thompson1971quadratic','chermak2004quadratic'],verification='indirect',reconstruction='Finiteness of the group, finite-dimensionality, exclusion of the identity as a generator, and the odd-prime convention are supplied from the published definition.')
item(192,'P000304','theorem','The central-product and Lie-type classification for odd primes at least five',r'For \(p>5\), the notes say that \(G\) is only a classical group, listing \(S,O\), and adding \ru{кроме \(E_8\)}.',r'For a quadratic pair with \(p\geq5\), the structural conclusion involves a central product of perfect groups with simple central quotients and a corresponding tensor decomposition of the module. In the quasisimple case the simple quotient is of Lie type in characteristic \(p\), from the families listed in Thompson\'s theorem, including exceptional families \(G_2,F_4,E_6,E_7\), but excluding \(E_8\). It is not a conclusion that \(G\) itself is a single classical simple group.',r'\citet[p.~375, Central product theorem and Main theorem]{thompson1971quadratic} gives precisely the missing structural qualifications. It was reported at Nice before the October seminar, although the proceedings were printed in 1971.',refs=['thompson1971quadratic'],literal='false as written',match='partial',verification='indirect',confidence='high',status='Established structural classification; the notebook\'s word classical and its omission of central products are not literal equivalents of the theorem.')
item(192,'P000304','question','The small-characteristic continuation of quadratic-pair theory',r'\ru{Остались хар-ки 2 и 3.}',r'The definite odd-characteristic continuation is to classify faithful irreducible quadratic pairs over \(F_3\). The characteristic-two phrase does not specify what additional restrictions distinguish the intended problem: in characteristic two every involution satisfies \((g-I)^2=0\).',r'For characteristic three, \citet[Theorems~A--B; introduction]{chermak2004quadratic} treats the component case using the finite-simple-group classification and points to the earlier component-free treatment. The opened preprint is dated February 2002; publication was 2004. Its introduction explains that the original program was displaced by a different route to the finite-simple-group classification.',refs=['chermak2004quadratic'],cls='B',reconstruction='The characteristic-three problem is separated from the underspecified characteristic-two slogan; no claim of solving every characteristic-two classification problem is made.',match='partial',problem='GP-1970-21',confidence='medium',status='The odd-characteristic quadratic-pair program has a classification-based treatment. The unqualified characteristic-two slogan is not assigned an exact solved theorem.')
item(192,'P000304-P000305','construction','The rootless even unimodular lattice in dimension twenty-four',r'Kirillov describes \(L\subset\mathbf R^{24}\) with covolume one, integral pairings, even squared norms, and minimum squared norm four, and says it is unique.',r'Up to Euclidean isometry there is a unique positive-definite even unimodular lattice of rank 24 with no norm-two vectors: the Leech lattice. Its minimum over nonzero vectors is four. The zero vector must be excluded from the minimum.',r'\citet[Theorem~1.1 and proof]{brouwerleech} reproduces Conway\'s uniqueness proof. The original 1969 characterization is identified there; this is prior work, not an unpublished seminar conjecture.',refs=['brouwerleech'],speaker='A. A. Kirillov',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',status='Established; uniqueness is up to isometry and the minimum is over nonzero lattice vectors.')
item(192,'P000304','claim','Conway\'s simple group requires a central quotient',r'The notes describe another finite simple group, Conway\'s, as the automorphism group of this lattice.',r'For the Leech lattice \(\Lambda\), \(\operatorname{Aut}(\Lambda)\) contains the central inversion \(-I\). The simple Conway group is \(\mathrm{Co}_1=\operatorname{Aut}(\Lambda)/\{\pm I\}\), not the full automorphism group.',r'The lattice automorphism group and its order occur in \citet[Theorem~1.1]{brouwerleech}; \citet[introduction, pp.~2--3]{chermak2004quadratic} explicitly identifies it as \(2\cdot\mathrm{Co}_1\). These confirm the missing central quotient independently of the notebook.',refs=['brouwerleech','chermak2004quadratic'],speaker='A. A. Kirillov',literal='false as written',match='partial',status='Established after inserting the quotient by central inversion.')
item(192,'P000305','remark','Divisibility of the signature for even unimodular lattices',r'\ru{Сигнатура дел. на 8 у полож. опр. форм.}',r'An even unimodular positive-definite integral lattice has rank, hence signature, divisible by eight. Positivity alone is insufficient: a one-dimensional positive form has signature one.',r'The lattice hypotheses in the surrounding passage supply the intended scope; \citet[Theorem~1.2(i)]{brouwerleech} states rank divisibility for even unimodular lattices.',refs=['brouwerleech'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',status='Classical theorem under evenness and unimodularity; false if read as an assertion about all positive-definite forms.')
problem('GP-1970-21','Quadratic pairs in characteristic three',r'Classify nontrivial finite groups \(G\) and faithful irreducible \(F_3G\)-modules generated by elements acting with \((g-I)^2=0\).',['1970-192.3'],r'Later classification work includes Chermak\'s 2002 preprint/2004 article, Theorems A--B, for the component case, together with its cited component-free treatment. The notebook\'s separate characteristic-two slogan has insufficient restrictions for the same identification.',['chermak2004quadratic'])
corr('P000304','Thompson classification',r'\(p>5\): only classical groups, except \(E_8\).',r'\(p\geq5\); central-product reduction, then a specified list of Lie-type simple quotients, including exceptional types other than \(E_8\).',r'The scan faithfully has \ru{классич.}; Thompson\'s ICM Main theorem includes \(G_2,F_4,E_6,E_7\), and the preceding Central product theorem supplies an omitted hypothesis/reduction.')
corr('P000304','Conway group',r'A finite simple group is the lattice automorphism group.',r'Take the quotient of \(\operatorname{Aut}(\Lambda)\) by its central \(\{\pm I\}\).',r'The scan makes no quotient visible. Central inversion is an immediate obstruction to literal simplicity; the opened Chermak preprint identifies the automorphism group as \(2\cdot\mathrm{Co}_1\).')
corr('P000305','Leech lattice minimum',r'\(\min(\lambda,\lambda)=4\).',r'\(\min_{0\ne\lambda\in\Lambda}(\lambda,\lambda)=4\).',r'The scan omits the range. The zero vector otherwise makes the minimum zero.')
corr('P000305','Signature remark',r'Every positive-definite form has signature divisible by eight.',r'The even integral unimodular lattice hypotheses must be retained.',r'The scan and Russian preserve only positivity in this sentence; a rank-one positive form is a counterexample to the unrestricted claim.')
talk(193,'1970-10-19','I. M. Gelfand','P000305','A proposed non-quasi-coherent sheaf and a localization reminder',r'This short intervention starts at the dated Gelfand heading and ends before Bernstein\'s example. Its proposed point-supported sheaf lacks enough module-structure data to justify the advertised counterexample.')
item(193,'P000305','construction','The proposed point-supported counterexample',r'A sheaf is said to have fibre \(A\) at one point \(x\in\operatorname{Spec}A\), zero elsewhere, and sections \(A\) exactly on opens containing \(x\); it is called non-quasi-coherent.',r'The formula defines a skyscraper sheaf of abelian groups. To make it an \(\mathcal O_X\)-module one must specify an \(\mathcal O_{X,x}=A_{\mathfrak p}\)-module structure on its value. Moreover, its stalk support is the closure of \(\{x\}\), so the description of its fibres requires \(x\) to be closed. A usual skyscraper \(i_*\kappa(x)\) at a closed point is quasi-coherent, and therefore does not give the claimed counterexample.',r'No exact well-posed non-quasi-coherent example is identifiable from these data. The affine module/sheaf correspondence in \citet[affine case]{serre1955coherent} explains why the standard closed-point skyscraper cannot support the claim. The missing action cannot be supplied from the word fibre alone.',refs=['serre1955coherent'],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='high',status='The intended example remains unidentified from the source; this is an identification limit, not a presently open theorem.')
item(193,'P000305','remark','Localization kills elements annihilated by the denominator',r'The boxed margin calculation is \(fm=0\Rightarrow m/f=mf/f^2=0\).',r'For any commutative ring \(A\), \(A\)-module \(M\), and \(f\in A\), an element \(m/f^r\in M_f\) is zero if and only if \(f^Nm=0\) for some \(N\geq0\). The displayed calculation is the case \(fm=0\).',r'This elementary localization fact underlies the denominator-clearing proof of affine acyclicity, given in \citet[Lemma~30.2.1]{stacks2026affine}. It is not an injectivity assertion for \(M\to M_f\).',refs=['stacks2026affine'],literal='correct')
corr('P000305','Proposed skyscraper example',r'Fibre \(A\) at \(x\), zero elsewhere; automatically a non-quasi-coherent sheaf of modules.',r'The \(A_{\mathfrak p}\)-action and closedness/support conditions must be specified; usual closed-point skyscrapers are quasi-coherent.',r'The scan contains no action or qualification. The correction concerns missing mathematical data, not an English mistranslation.',typ='ambiguity')

talk(194,'1970-10-19','I. N. Bernstein','P000305-P000306','Regular functions on affine varieties and the module/sheaf correspondence',r'Bernstein\'s example is followed through its ideal-sheaf sequence, the supported-section gluing argument, and the presentation argument for quasi-coherent sheaves. The distinction between one global denominator and a locally regular function is made explicitly in the manuscript.')
item(194,'P000305-P000306','theorem','A globally defined rational expression on an affine variety is polynomial modulo its ideal',r'If \(P/Q\) is defined everywhere on the subvariety corresponding to \(I\subset\mathbf C[x_1,\ldots,x_n]\), then \(P/Q=R\pmod I\) for a polynomial \(R\).',r'Let \(A=\mathbf C[x_1,\ldots,x_n]\), and suppose \(Q\) has no zero on \(V(I)\). Then \((I,Q)=A\) by the Nullstellensatz; hence \(Q\) is invertible in \(A/I\) and \(P/Q\) is the image of a polynomial. More generally, the ring of global regular functions on the affine scheme \(\operatorname{Spec}(A/I)\) is \(A/I\).',r'This is a standard affine consequence of the module/sheaf correspondence in \citet[affine case]{serre1955coherent}; the explicit Nullstellensatz proof is resumed on P000309.',refs=['serre1955coherent'],reconstruction=r'For the single-fraction proof, defined means that the chosen denominator \(Q\) is nowhere zero, not merely that a removable singularity can be canceled locally.')
item(194,'P000305-P000306','construction','Ideal sheaves and the closed affine subscheme',r'An ideal \(J\subset A\) gives \(\mathcal F_J\subset\mathcal O_X\); the quotient sheaf is associated with \(A/J\), and its support is the subvariety.',r'Sheafification on \(X=\operatorname{Spec}A\) is exact. Thus \(0\to\widetilde J\to\mathcal O_X\to\widetilde{A/J}\to0\) is exact and \(\widetilde{A/J}=i_*\mathcal O_{\operatorname{Spec}(A/J)}\) for the closed immersion \(i\). Prime ideals, not only complex closed points, are included in \(X\).',r'Classical affine scheme theory, as in \citet[affine case]{serre1955coherent}; arbitrary-ring affine acyclicity is verified in \citet[Lemma~30.2.2]{stacks2026affine}.',refs=['serre1955coherent','stacks2026affine'],literal='correct')
item(194,'P000306','method','Gluing a supported section before taking global sections',r'The notes glue \(P/Q\) on the locus where \(Q\ne0\) to zero on the complement of the subvariety.',r'In \(\widetilde{A/I}\), which vanishes away from \(V(I)\), the two sections agree on the overlap. Their glued section over all of \(\operatorname{Spec}A\) lies in \(\Gamma(X,\widetilde{A/I})=A/I\). This gluing must be performed in the quotient sheaf, not in \(\mathcal O_X\).',r'The argument is a concrete use of the affine correspondence, not a new extension theorem; compare \citet[affine case]{serre1955coherent}.',refs=['serre1955coherent'],reconstruction='The sheaf in which the two local sections are compared is made explicit.')
item(194,'P000306','theorem','Recovering a quasi-coherent sheaf from its global module',r'A presentation by sums of \(\mathcal O_X\) is compared with the corresponding module presentation, concluding \(\mathcal F=\mathcal F_M\).',r'For every affine scheme \(X=\operatorname{Spec}A\), the functors \(M\mapsto\widetilde M\) and \(\mathcal F\mapsto\Gamma(X,\mathcal F)\) are inverse equivalences between \(A\)-modules and quasi-coherent \(\mathcal O_X\)-modules. Presentations can use arbitrary direct sums; finite presentations require the corresponding finite-presentation hypothesis.',r'The coherent affine case predates the seminar in \citet{serre1955coherent}; the standard arbitrary-ring formulation is compatible with \citet[Lemma~30.2.2 and its preceding section]{stacks2026affine}.',refs=['serre1955coherent','stacks2026affine'])

talk(195,'1970-10-19','Ya. G. Sinai','P000307-P000308','States of a one-dimensional quantum spin chain',r'The lecture constructs the quasi-local matrix algebra, states and their density matrices, diagonal classical states, finite-temperature Gibbs limits, and the GNS representation. The existence and factor questions are revisited in records 197 and 200.')
item(195,'P000307','construction','The quasi-local spin algebra',r'Two-dimensional spaces \(H_i\), tensor products \(H_I\), matrix rings \(\mathcal A(I)\), and inclusions by tensoring with the identity are introduced.',r'For finite \(I\subset\mathbf Z\), set \(H_I=\bigotimes_{i\in I}\mathbf C^2\) and \(\mathcal A(I)=B(H_I)\). The compatible embeddings \(a\mapsto a\otimes1\) give the unital UHF \(C^*\)-algebra \(\mathcal A=\overline{\bigcup_I\mathcal A(I)}^{\|\cdot\|}\). In particular \(\dim H_I=2^{|I|}\), not \(2|I|\).',r'This uniformly hyperfinite setup is used in \citet[section~2]{powers1967representations}. It was standard before the lecture.',refs=['powers1967representations'],literal='correct')
item(195,'P000307','definition','States and local density matrices',r'A positive functional \(\varphi\) with \(\varphi(1)=1\) restricts to \(\operatorname{Tr}(a\rho_I)\) on each local matrix algebra.',r'A state is a positive complex-linear functional of norm one. Every restriction to \(\mathcal A(I)\) has a unique density matrix \(\rho_I\geq0\), \(\operatorname{Tr}\rho_I=1\); consistency means \(\operatorname{Tr}_{J\setminus I}\rho_J=\rho_I\) for \(I\subset J\). Conversely a compatible family defines a state on \(\mathcal A\).',r'The finite-dimensional trace representation and the UHF state construction are the framework of \citet[section~2]{powers1967representations}.',refs=['powers1967representations'],literal='correct')
item(195,'P000307','construction','Product-diagonal states and cylinder measures',r'In a fixed sitewise basis the eigenvalues of the density matrices are treated as joint probabilities.',r'Choose an orthonormal basis at each site. Compatible density matrices diagonal in all resulting tensor bases are equivalent to probability measures on \(\{1,2\}^{\mathbf Z}\). For a finite word \(\alpha\), its probability is \(\varphi(\bigotimes_{i\in I}|e^i_{\alpha_i}\rangle\langle e^i_{\alpha_i}|)\). It is not the value of \(\varphi\) on a tensor product of vectors.',r'This is the commutative cylinder-measure construction inside the diagonal subalgebra of the UHF algebra. Product states are examined in \citet[section~4]{powers1967representations}. Record 197 attempts a broader commutation criterion, which fails without additional hypotheses.',refs=['powers1967representations'],reconstruction='Rank-one projections replace the notebook\'s vector expression so that the state is applied to an element of its algebra.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
item(195,'P000308','construction','Finite-volume Gibbs functionals',r'A translated nearest-neighbor operator is summed to form a Hamiltonian, and normalized traces of \(ae^{-\beta H_n}\) are taken.',r'For a self-adjoint nearest-neighbor interaction \(h\in\mathcal A(\{0,1\})\), real finite \(\beta\), and a finite interval \(\Lambda\), put \(H_\Lambda=\sum_{\{j,j+1\}\subset\Lambda}\tau_j(h)\). The state is \(\varphi_\Lambda(a)=\operatorname{Tr}(ae^{-\beta H_\Lambda})/\operatorname{Tr}(e^{-\beta H_\Lambda})\) on \(\mathcal A(\Lambda)\).',r'The finite-range spin-chain Gibbs construction is treated in \citet{araki1969gibbs}. Positivity requires a self-adjoint Hamiltonian; no arbitrary matrix \(h\) suffices.',refs=['araki1969gibbs'],verification='publisher metadata checked')
item(195,'P000308','theorem','The one-dimensional Gibbs limit',r'The notes assert convergence \(\varphi_n(a)\to\varphi(a)\) for every quasi-local observable.',r'For a translation-invariant finite-range self-adjoint interaction on a one-dimensional quantum spin lattice, the finite-temperature thermodynamic limit with the usual free finite-volume boundary convention exists. The resulting state is primary; the finite-dimensional local state spaces and finite interaction range are part of the assertion.',r'\citet[abstract]{araki1969gibbs}, published in 1969, states existence of the infinite-volume primary Gibbs state, before Sinai\'s lecture. This is an exact match for the finite-range one-dimensional situation, not an assertion about arbitrary dimension or long-range interactions.',refs=['araki1969gibbs'],verification='publisher metadata checked',status='Established for the stated one-dimensional finite-range finite-temperature system.')
item(195,'P000308','remark','The analogy with Markov chains',r'The Gibbs construction is called a noncommutative analogue of Markov chains.',r'The nearest-neighbor energy resembles the classical one-step Markov/Gibbs construction. For noncommuting quantum interactions this analogy alone does not assert a conditional-independence identity, a transition operator, or the modern quantum Markov property.',r'\citet{araki1969gibbs} supplies a rigorous quantum Gibbs theory. No exact quantum Markov theorem is stated or proved in the notebook passage; the publication match is thematic.',refs=['araki1969gibbs'],match='thematic only',verification='publisher metadata checked',literal='correct',confidence='high',status='A useful structural analogy, with no additional Markov theorem inferred.')
item(195,'P000308','construction','The GNS Hilbert space and generated von Neumann algebra',r'\((a,b)=\varphi(b^*a)\); factor by null vectors, complete, and let the algebra act by left multiplication.',r'Let \(N_\varphi=\{a:\varphi(a^*a)=0\}\). Complete \(\mathcal A/N_\varphi\) in the inner product \(\langle[a],[b]\rangle=\varphi(b^*a)\). Left multiplication gives a bounded representation \(\pi_\varphi\), since \(\|\pi_\varphi(x)\|\leq\|x\|\), and \(M_\varphi=\pi_\varphi(\mathcal A)^{\prime\prime}\). The null space is a left ideal; it need not be a two-sided ideal.',r'The construction and factor representations are used throughout \citet{powers1967representations}; the notebook cites the classical Gelfand--Naimark tradition. This is prior foundational theory.',refs=['powers1967representations'],literal='correct')
item(195,'P000308','question','When is the GNS algebra a factor, and what type occurs?',r'The notes ask when the generated ring is a factor and emphasize factors of type III.',r'The factor question is whether \(Z(M_\varphi)=\mathbf C1\). It is separate from the type of a factor. States of the same UHF algebra can yield type I, type II, or type III factors; the phrase principally type III is a motivation, not a theorem about every state.',r'\citet[Theorem~2.5]{powers1967representations} gives the factor criterion later explained in record 200; section~4 exhibits the different types for product states. In particular the tracial product state yields type \(\mathrm{II}_1\).',refs=['powers1967representations'],problem='GP-1970-22',status='The factor criterion and important examples were established before the seminar; no universal type-III assertion is valid.')
corr('P000307','Cylinder probability',r'\(\varphi(e_{\alpha_1}^{i_1}\otimes\cdots\otimes e_{\alpha_n}^{i_n})\).',r'Apply \(\varphi\) to the tensor product of the associated rank-one projections.',r'\(\varphi\) is defined on matrices, not on Hilbert-space vectors. This is a typing correction to the mathematical abbreviation.')

talk(196,'1970-10-26','D. A. Kazhdan','P000309-P000311','Affine acyclicity by localization and Čech cochains',r'The proof works with a finite distinguished-open cover. It includes both an explicit denominator-clearing attempt and Bernstein\'s annihilator argument; the manuscript itself acknowledges that localization equalities need more care.')
item(196,'P000309','theorem','Vanishing of higher cohomology on an affine scheme',r'For an affine scheme \(X\) and a quasi-coherent sheaf \(\mathcal F\), \(H^q(X,\mathcal F)=0\), \(q>0\).',r'For every commutative ring \(A\), every \(A\)-module \(M\), and \(q>0\), \(H^q(\operatorname{Spec}A,\widetilde M)=0\). Neither Noetherianity nor finite generation of \(M\) is required.',r'The coherent algebraic-geometric theorem is in \citet{serre1955coherent}. The exact arbitrary-ring formulation and proof are \citet[Lemma~30.2.2]{stacks2026affine}.',refs=['serre1955coherent','stacks2026affine'],literal='correct')
item(196,'P000309','method','The Nullstellensatz proof of Bernstein\'s rational-function example',r'For generators \(F_i\) of the ideal and a nowhere-zero \(Q\), find \(L_0Q+\sum_iL_iF_i=P\).',r'If \((Q,F_1,\ldots,F_r)\) has no common zero over an algebraically closed field, it is the unit ideal. Multiplying a representation of one by \(P\) produces the displayed identity and \(P/Q\equiv L_0\pmod I\).',r'This proves the single-denominator instance of 1970-194.1 by elementary affine algebra; it does not need the cohomology theorem subsequently being proved. Compare \citet[affine case]{serre1955coherent}.',refs=['serre1955coherent'],literal='correct')
item(196,'P000309-P000310','construction','The localization Čech complex',r'For \(X_i=D(f_i)\) covering \(\operatorname{Spec}A\), cochains have components in \(M_{f_{i_0}\cdots f_{i_q}}\), with alternating restriction differential.',r'Choose \(f_1,\ldots,f_r\) generating the unit ideal. The augmented complex starts \(M\to\prod_i M_{f_i}\to\prod_{i<j}M_{f_if_j}\to\cdots\). With increasing indices, \((dc)_{i_0\cdots i_{q+1}}=\sum_{s=0}^{q+1}(-1)^s c_{i_0\cdots\widehat{i_s}\cdots i_{q+1}}\) after restriction.',r'\citet[Lemma~30.2.1]{stacks2026affine} gives exactness for this finite principal cover and identifies the finite localization argument.',refs=['stacks2026affine'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(196,'P000310','method','Alternating cochains and the failed single-chart contraction',r'The notes pass to skew-symmetric cochains; for a one-cocycle they first try one distinguished index, then note that \(m_{1i}\) may have an unwanted denominator.',r'The normalized alternating Čech complex computes the same cohomology as the full ordered-index complex. A contraction based on a fixed chart is valid after localizing where that chart covers everything, but \(m_{1i}\in M_{f_1f_i}\) need not already lie in \(M_{f_i}\). The latter obstruction invalidates a global single-chart primitive.',r'The localization contraction in \citet[proof of Lemma~30.2.1]{stacks2026affine} makes precisely this distinction. The notebook records the failed step as part of a proof search, not a completed proof.',refs=['stacks2026affine'],literal='correct',match='partial')
item(196,'P000311','method','Uniform denominator clearing and a partition of unity in the ring',r'Write \(m_{ij}=\bar m_{ij}/(f_i^Nf_j^N)\), choose \(\sum_iL_if_i^N=1\), and sum over \(i\).',r'After increasing a common power enough to clear all denominators and all finitely many cocycle equalities, a ring identity \(\sum_i L_if_i^N=1\) gives a primitive. For the convention \(m_{ij}=n_i-n_j\), one can set \(n_i=\sum_k L_k f_k^N m_{ik}\) in \(M_{f_i}\), after the clearing step. Equality in a localization may require an additional power before it holds in an earlier module.',r'This is the standard algebraic proof behind \citet[Lemma~30.2.1]{stacks2026affine}. The corrected formula removes an extraneous denominator from one displayed intermediate equality on P000311; the later lines of the manuscript have the intended sum.',refs=['stacks2026affine'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated')
item(196,'P000311','method','Bernstein\'s annihilator proof of affine acyclicity',r'Each class is killed by a power of every \(f_i\), and those powers still generate the unit ideal.',r'Localizing the Čech complex at \(f_i\) makes its cover contain the whole affine scheme and hence contracts it. For any given higher-cohomology class \(c\), there are powers with \(f_i^{N_i}c=0\). Finiteness of the cover gives a common \(N\); since \((f_1^N,\ldots,f_r^N)=A\), one obtains \(c=0\).',r'\citet[proof of Lemma~30.2.1]{stacks2026affine} presents the same localization-to-vanishing mechanism. The argument is valid for arbitrary modules.',refs=['stacks2026affine'],speaker='I. N. Bernstein',literal='correct')
corr('P000310','Čech differential target indices',r'The left side of the differential formula omits the final target index.',r'\((dc)_{i_0\ldots i_{q+1}}\).',r'The right side has \(q+2\) alternating faces; a degree-\(q+1\) cochain has \(q+2\) indices.')
corr('P000311','Partition-of-unity coefficients',r'\(L_i\in X_i\).',r'\(L_i\in A\).',r'The scan visibly has \(X_i\), faithfully transcribed. \(X_i\) is an open set, whereas the identity \(\sum L_if_i^N=1\) requires ring coefficients.')
corr('P000311','Middle equality of the primitive calculation',r'An additional prefactor \(1/(f_{i_0}^Nf_{i_1}^N)\) multiplies already localized cocycle terms.',r'Drop the additional prefactor, keeping the identity \(\sum_i L_i f_i^N(m_{i_0i}-m_{i_1i})=m_{i_0i_1}\).',r'Scan checked: the extra factor is present in the intermediate line, but disappears in the next line. Direct substitution into the displayed fractions detects the extra denominator.')

talk(197,'1970-10-26','Ya. G. Sinai','P000312-P000313','Commuting densities, classical measures, and a failed diagonalization claim',r'The quantum-state construction is repeated, now with partial traces. The assertion that mutually commuting embedded marginals admit a fixed sitewise product eigenbasis fails in the presence of degeneracies; an explicit faithful counterexample is supplied in the chapter\'s audit annotation.')
item(197,'P000312','construction','Tensor-product local systems and compatible density operators',r'The first display has \(H_I=\bigoplus_{i\in I}H_i\), but the basis, algebra embeddings and partial trace formulas use tensor products.',r'The intended local Hilbert space is \(H_I=\bigotimes_{i\in I}H_i\), with \(\mathcal A(J)=\mathcal A(I)\otimes\mathcal A(J\setminus I)\) and \(\operatorname{Tr}_{J\setminus I}\rho_J=\rho_I\). This restates 1970-195.1--2.',r'The UHF framework in \citet[section~2]{powers1967representations} fixes the notation. The direct-sum sign is a faithful manuscript slip, not a valid alternative construction.',refs=['powers1967representations'],literal='false as written',development='annotated')
item(197,'P000312-P000313','claim','Commuting finite marginals need not have a common product eigenbasis',r'A state is called of measure type when all embedded \(\rho_I\) commute. The next page asserts a common tensor basis of eigenvectors of the one-site densities, explicitly noting that the argument is obvious for simple spectra.',r'Product diagonality implies commutation. The converse is valid if every one-site density has simple spectrum: commuting with each local one-site density forces diagonalization in their product basis. Without that hypothesis, pairwise commutation of all embedded finite marginals is insufficient, even when every finite marginal is strictly positive.',r'The implication fails by the faithful Bell-pair construction checked in this chapter\'s quantum-state annotation. The product-diagonal construction remains valid; \citet[section~4]{powers1967representations} treats product-state representations but does not supply the false general implication.',refs=['powers1967representations'],literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated',status='False without the missing nondegeneracy or another hypothesis ensuring a product eigenbasis; it is not an open problem.',reconstruction=r'The notebook\'s commutation definition and the stronger product-diagonal property are kept separate. A repair by simple one-site spectra is explicitly conditional.')
item(197,'P000313','construction','Consistent eigenvalue tables and the infinite product probability space',r'The sums \(\sum_{\alpha_n}\mu(\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_n)=\mu(\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_{n-1})\) define a cylinder measure on \(\prod_i\mathbf Z_2\); Haar measure is an example.',r'When a fixed product basis really diagonalizes the densities, partial traces give consistent finite probability tables. The resulting Borel probability measure on \(\{0,1\}^{\mathbf Z}\) is unique. The product of equal weights is the Haar probability measure on the compact product group \(\prod_{i\in\mathbf Z}\mathbf Z/2\mathbf Z\).',r'This is the valid classical part of 1970-195.3. For the Haar example the corresponding quantum state is tracial, so \citet[section~4]{powers1967representations} identifies a type \(\mathrm{II}_1\), rather than type III, factor.',refs=['powers1967representations'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
item(197,'P000313','construction','GNS representation and the role of type III factors',r'The positive form, quotient, completion, and left multiplication are repeated, followed by the statement that type III factors are important.',r'The GNS construction in 1970-195.7 applies to all these states. The study of type III factors motivates selecting nontracial infinite product and Gibbs states; it does not classify every state constructed on P000312--313 as type III.',r'\citet[section~4]{powers1967representations} constructs type III product-state factors and distinguishes the pure and tracial endpoints. This supplies the prior mathematical background without converting a motivational remark into a universal theorem.',refs=['powers1967representations'],literal='correct',match='partial',problem='GP-1970-22')
corr('P000312','Local Hilbert-space display',r'\(H_I=\bigoplus_{i\in I}H_i\).',r'\(H_I=\bigotimes_{i\in I}H_i\).',r'The scan has the direct-sum-like circled plus. The adjacent tensor basis, \(a\otimes1\) inclusion, and partial trace require tensor products.')
corr('P000313','Product eigenbasis assertion',r'Commuting embedded densities always admit a product eigenbasis.',r'This follows under simple one-site spectra; it is false in general with degeneracies.',r'The scan explicitly mentions simple spectrum as the obvious case, then asserts existence more generally. The checked faithful Bell-pair counterexample refutes that extension.')
bib('gelfandponomarev1971quadruples','article',dict(author="Gel'fand, I. M. and Ponomarev, V. A.",title='Quadruples of subspaces of a finite-dimensional vector space',journal='Doklady Akademii Nauk SSSR',year=1971,volume=197,number=4,pages='762--765'),'https://www.mathnet.ru/eng/dan36010','Metadata and full four-page Russian paper opened via its PDF link. Sections 1--4, Claims 1--2 and Theorem 1 read. Received 21 October 1970. The original works over an algebraically closed characteristic-zero field. It contains the exact canonical forms but not the seminar stability example.')
bib('enomotowatatani2004subspaces','misc',dict(author='Enomoto, Masatoshi and Watatani, Yasuo',title='Relative position of four subspaces in a Hilbert space',year=2004,eprint='math/0404545',archivePrefix='arXiv'),'https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0404545','Author preprint version 2, 16 August 2004, opened. Finite-dimensional Section 9, Theorems 9.1--9.2 and canonical forms on pp. 21--23 read; introduction graph encoding and Section 7 also checked. This reference is used for its explicit finite-dimensional results, not to identify the notebook with an infinite-dimensional problem.')
bib('bernsteingelfandponomarev1973coxeter','article',dict(author='Bernstein, I. N. and Gelfand, I. M. and Ponomarev, V. A.',title="Coxeter functors and Gabriel's theorem",journal='Russian Mathematical Surveys',year=1973,volume=28,number=2,pages='17--32'),'https://www.math.tau.ac.il/~bernstei/Publication_list/publication_texts/BGG-CoxeterF-Usp.pdf','Full English paper opened from the author webpage. Introduction and final four-subspace discussion, pp. 30--32, read. Received 18 December 1972. Used for the quiver translation and distinction between regular families and rigid cases, not as a source for an unlocated orbit-degeneration formula.')
bib('stacks2026picardcurves','misc',dict(author='{The Stacks Project Authors}',title='Picard groups of curves',year=2026,howpublished='The Stacks Project, Tag 03RN'),'https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03RN','Section 59.69 and the proof of Lemma 59.69.3 read. It gives Picard group of a punctured smooth projective curve as the quotient by the classes of omitted points.')
bib('deligne1971hodge','article',dict(author='Deligne, Pierre',title=r'Th\'eorie de {Hodge}, {II}',journal=r'Publications Math\'ematiques de l\'IH\'ES',year=1971,volume=40,pages='5--57',doi='10.1007/BF02684692'),'https://www.numdam.org/article/PMIHES_1971__40__5_0.pdf','Original full paper opened. Proposition 3.1.8 on the logarithmic de Rham comparison and Theorem 3.2.5 on independence of compactification read. Received 15 September 1970; publication 1971. The received date does not by itself establish public circulation.')

talk(198,'1970-11-02','I. M. Gelfand','P000314-P000317','Four subspaces: classification, normal forms, and deformation',r'Gelfand reports joint work with V. A. Ponomarev. The graph examples and most displayed normal forms match the 1971 Doklady announcement very closely. The defect sign, missing odd-dimensional defect-zero systems, and broad stability assertions require separate audit; the classification itself is established.')
item(198,'P000314','definition','The category of ordered four-subspace systems',r'\(S=(P;E_1,E_2,E_3,E_4)\), with ordered subspaces and morphisms carrying each \(E_i\) into its counterpart; direct sums and permutations are introduced.',r'Objects consist of a finite-dimensional vector space \(P\) over \(k\) and four ordered subspaces. A morphism \(u:S\to S^{\prime}\) satisfies \(u(E_i)\subset E_i^{\prime}\); an isomorphism is an invertible \(u\) carrying each subspace onto the corresponding subspace. Direct sums are componentwise. Permuting labels is a separate action, not part of ordered isomorphism.',r'Exactly \citet[section~1, p.~762]{gelfandponomarev1971quadruples}. The announcement was received 21 October 1970 and printed in 1971.',refs=['gelfandponomarev1971quadruples'],cls='A',literal='correct')
item(198,'P000314','construction','The four-armed quiver and its injectivity condition',r'The category is said to be equivalent to diagrams with four arrows directed into one central vertex.',r'Four-subspace systems identify with representations of the inward-oriented affine \(D_4\) quiver in which all four arrow maps are injective. Arbitrary representations also include kernels at outer vertices and hence are not literally the same category. The diagrammatic language unifies several matrix classification problems.',r'The subsequent quiver framework is explicit in \citet[introduction and final example]{bernsteingelfandponomarev1973coxeter}; compare \citet[section~1]{gelfandponomarev1971quadruples}.',refs=['gelfandponomarev1971quadruples','bernsteingelfandponomarev1973coxeter'],cls='A',reconstruction='Injectivity of the four arrows is supplied; it is forced by calling their domains subspaces.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(198,'P000314','construction','Encoding similarity of an operator by four subspaces',r'In \(E\oplus E\), take the two coordinate summands, the graph of \(A:E\to E\), and the diagonal.',r'The quadruple \(S_A=(E\oplus E;E\oplus0,0\oplus E,\operatorname{graph}A,\Delta_E)\) is isomorphic to \(S_B\) exactly when \(A\) and \(B\) are similar. The first two subspaces force a block diagonal map and the diagonal forces the two blocks to agree. Direct sum and indecomposability correspond to those of operators.',r'The identical construction is \citet[section~1, example~1]{gelfandponomarev1971quadruples}; the equivalence is also stated in \citet[p.~2]{enomotowatatani2004subspaces}.',refs=['gelfandponomarev1971quadruples','enomotowatatani2004subspaces'],cls='A',literal='correct')
item(198,'P000314','construction','Opposite maps, matrix pencils, and linear relations',r'The notes mention \(A:E\to F\), \(B:F\to E\), Kronecker\'s problem, and relations regarded as arbitrary graphs.',r'In \(E\oplus F\), take the two coordinate subspaces together with \(\{(x,Ax)\}\) and \(\{(By,y)\}\). Two parallel maps \(E\to F\) instead give two ordinary graphs and encode a matrix pencil. Replacing graphs by arbitrary subspaces of \(E\oplus F\) encodes linear relations; a relation need not be the graph of a single-valued map.',r'These are exactly the five examples in \citet[section~1, pp.~762--763; section~4]{gelfandponomarev1971quadruples}. The article explains that the four-subspace problem includes, but is broader than, pairs of linear relations.',refs=['gelfandponomarev1971quadruples'],cls='A',literal='correct',reconstruction='The three related encodings are written separately rather than treating the word graph as imposing single-valuedness.')
item(198,'P000314-P000316','construction','Four lines and the cross-ratio parameter',r'Four lines in a plane have one invariant \(\lambda\), with its transformations under relabeling.',r'For four distinct ordered lines in a two-dimensional vector space over an algebraically closed field, normalize three projective points to \(0,1,\infty\). The fourth gives \(\lambda\notin\{0,1\}\). Relabeling gives the six values \(\lambda,1-\lambda,1/\lambda,1/(1-\lambda),\lambda/(\lambda-1),(\lambda-1)/\lambda\), with coincidences at special values.',r'This is the \(k=1\) parameter family in \citet[section~2]{gelfandponomarev1971quadruples}. The P000316 list, and even the compressed list in the announcement, omit one of the six cross-ratio transforms; the earlier P000314 generators recover it.',refs=['gelfandponomarev1971quadruples'],cls='E',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',development='annotated')
item(198,'P000315','theorem','Indecomposable classification and the five possible defects',r'Every system decomposes into indecomposables of defect \(0,\pm1,\pm2\); the boxed definition is \(2\dim P-\sum_i\dim E_i\).',r'Over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, every finite four-subspace system is a direct sum of the canonical indecomposables in the Gelfand--Ponomarev list. With the published convention \(\delta(S)=\sum_i\dim E_i-2\dim P\), indecomposable defects belong to \(\{-2,-1,0,1,2\}\). The notebook\'s boxed definition is \(-\delta\), while its subsequent labels use \(\delta\).',r'\citet[Claims~1--2 and Theorem~1, pp.~764--765]{gelfandponomarev1971quadruples} gives the classification; \citet[Theorems~9.1--9.2]{enomotowatatani2004subspaces} reproduces the complete list. The original received date is before this lecture, but printing is 1971.',refs=['gelfandponomarev1971quadruples','enomotowatatani2004subspaces'],cls='A',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated',status='Classified; the defect sign must be normalized before using the listed labels.')
item(198,'P000315','construction','The two even-dimensional defect-one normal forms',r'For \(P=\langle e_1,\ldots,e_k,f_1,\ldots,f_k\rangle\), the first system has \(E_3=\langle e_2+f_1,\ldots,e_k+f_{k-1}\rangle\); the second adjoins \(e_1,f_k\).',r'In both forms \(E_1=\langle e_i\rangle\), \(E_2=\langle f_i\rangle\), and \(E_4=\langle e_i+f_i\rangle\). The first has \(\dim E_3=k-1\) and published defect \(-1\), the second \(\dim E_3=k+1\) and defect \(+1\). Label permutations give the other distinguished-index forms.',r'The displayed bases match \citet[section~2, p.~763]{gelfandponomarev1971quadruples}. Only the notebook\'s preceding definition reverses the defect sign.',refs=['gelfandponomarev1971quadruples'],cls='A',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated')
item(198,'P000315-P000316','construction','The exceptional and one-parameter even-dimensional defect-zero forms',r'One form takes \(E_3=\langle e_1,e_2+f_1,\ldots,e_k+f_{k-1}\rangle\); the other has a Jordan-chain parameter \(\lambda\ne0,1\).',r'Keep \(E_1,E_2,E_4\) as in 1970-198.7. The exceptional form has the stated \(E_3\); its permutations give \(S_{i,j}(2k,0)\). The family \(S(2k,0;\lambda)\) has \(E_3=\langle e_1+\lambda f_1,e_2+f_1+\lambda f_2,\ldots,e_k+f_{k-1}+\lambda f_k\rangle\), \(\lambda\in k\setminus\{0,1\}\). The excluded parameters belong to exceptional label families, not to additional generic families.',r'\citet[section~2, p.~763 and Claim~1]{gelfandponomarev1971quadruples} gives these exact forms and their permutation rules.',refs=['gelfandponomarev1971quadruples'],cls='A',literal='correct')
item(198,'P000316','construction','Odd-dimensional defect-two forms and duality',r'The notes write \(S(2k-1,2)\) explicitly and obtain \(S(2k-1,-2)\) by taking orthogonals.',r'For \(k\geq2\), in dimension \(2k-1\), the displayed four subspaces each have dimension \(k\), so their published defect is \(+2\). Their annihilators in \(P^*\) have dimension \(k-1\), hence defect \(-2\). Dual annihilators are the field-independent meaning of orthogonals; a chosen inner product may identify them with orthogonal complements. The one-dimensional endpoints are handled separately.',r'The bases coincide with \citet[section~2, p.~764]{gelfandponomarev1971quadruples}; see also \citet[section~9, forms~8--9]{enomotowatatani2004subspaces}.',refs=['gelfandponomarev1971quadruples','enomotowatatani2004subspaces'],cls='A',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(198,'P000316','claim','The notebook incorrectly excludes odd-dimensional defect zero',r'\ru{def 0 в неч.-мерном не бывает.}',r'Odd-dimensional indecomposables of defect zero do exist. For example \((k;k,0,k,0)\) in ambient dimension one is indecomposable and has defect zero. More generally the published list contains \(S_{i,j}(2k-1,0)\). The manuscript omits these forms, together with its full odd-dimensional defect-one list.',r'\citet[Claim~2, p.~764]{gelfandponomarev1971quadruples} explicitly includes the missing odd families. The counterexample follows directly from the definition, with no unresolved classification issue.',refs=['gelfandponomarev1971quadruples'],cls='A',literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated',status='The literal exclusion is false; the published classification supplies the omitted families.')
item(198,'P000316-P000317','claim','Parameters and local rigidity are not interchangeable',r'General-position systems are said to have no parameter; parameter-free indecomposables are said always to be stable under deformation.',r'For fixed dimensions, local rigidity means that the \(\mathrm{GL}(P)\)-orbit is open in the product of Grassmannians. Four distinct lines in a plane form a general-position family with varying cross ratio. A finite exceptional list does not by itself imply open orbits: exceptional defect-zero systems can deform into the parameter family.',r'The explicit families and exceptional specializations in \citet[section~2]{gelfandponomarev1971quadruples} already disprove the unrestricted parameter/general-position slogan. The regular-versus-irregular distinction in \citet[final example]{bernsteingelfandponomarev1973coxeter} is the appropriate later framework; it does not identify every parameter-free case as rigid.',refs=['gelfandponomarev1971quadruples','bernsteingelfandponomarev1973coxeter'],literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated',status='False in the stated generality; rigidity requires an orbit or deformation calculation.')
item(198,'P000317','question','A direct sum of rigid systems need not be rigid',r'\(S(5,2)\) and \(S(1,2)\) are called stable, but their sum deforms to \(S(3,2)\oplus S(3,2)\); a simple proof is requested.',r'Using the published defect convention, all three terms are the defect-two canonical systems; both sums have ambient dimension six and four subspaces of dimension four. The intended assertion is that arbitrarily small deformations of \(S(5,2)\oplus S(1,2)\) have the isomorphism type \(S(3,2)^{\oplus2}\). Thus the latter is the generic nearby orbit; the former lies in its orbit closure.',r'The canonical systems are identified exactly in \citet[section~2]{gelfandponomarev1971quadruples}, but the opened announcement and later classification do not state this precise degeneration. The classification match alone is not treated as verification of the orbit-closure assertion or of a published simple proof.',refs=['gelfandponomarev1971quadruples','enomotowatatani2004subspaces'],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='partial',verification='unresolved lead',problem='GP-1970-23',confidence='medium',status='A precise finite-dimensional deformation question is identifiable; the exact historical proof and publication match were not verified in this search. No assertion of present openness is made.')
item(198,'P000317','theorem','Uniqueness of decomposition into indecomposables',r'The last parenthesis says that decomposition is unique if the indecomposables are distinct and that the number is always the same.',r'The finite-dimensional category has the Krull--Schmidt property: the multiset of indecomposable isomorphism classes, with multiplicities, is unique up to permutation. Distinctness of summands is unnecessary. Existence follows by decreasing ambient dimension; uniqueness uses finite-dimensional endomorphism algebras and the usual local-endomorphism argument.',r'The direct-sum classification in \citet[Theorem~1]{gelfandponomarev1971quadruples}, viewed in the representation category of \citet{bernsteingelfandponomarev1973coxeter}, is a classical Krull--Schmidt setting.',refs=['gelfandponomarev1971quadruples','bernsteingelfandponomarev1973coxeter'],cls='E',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
problem('GP-1970-23','Elementary proof of the four-subspace deformation example',r'Give a direct proof that \(S(5,2)\oplus S(1,2)\) has arbitrarily small deformations of type \(S(3,2)\oplus S(3,2)\), although the two individual summands are rigid.',['1970-198.12'],r'The normal forms are identified in the 1971 classification. A publication containing this exact deformation or its requested elementary proof was not verified; this entry records an identification limit and does not declare the question open.',['gelfandponomarev1971quadruples','enomotowatatani2004subspaces'])
corr('P000314','Quiver equivalence',r'All vector-space diagrams of the displayed four-armed shape.',r'The full subcategory with injective arrows into the central vertex.',r'Subspace inclusions are injective; an arbitrary diagram may have outer-vertex kernels and is not literally an ordered-subspace system.')
corr('P000315','Definition of defect',r'\(\rho=2\dim P-\sum_i\dim E_i\), with subsequent labels \(S_3(2k,-1)\), \(S_3(2k,1)\).',r'Either negate every displayed defect label, or use the published convention \(\delta=\sum_i\dim E_i-2\dim P\).',r'Scan confirms the boxed sign. Counting the dimensions of the very next two displayed bases gives the reverse labels; the original 1971 paper uses the latter convention.')
corr('P000316','Anharmonic orbit of the cross ratio',r'Five listed transformations.',r'Include \(\lambda/(\lambda-1)\) as the sixth, allowing coincidences at special values.',r'The scan has only five entries. The earlier P000314 generators include the missing transform. The 1971 announcement repeats the compressed five-entry list, so this is not solely an OCR error.')
corr('P000316','Odd-dimensional classification',r'Defect zero does not occur in odd dimension.',r'Odd-dimensional exceptional defect-zero indecomposables occur.',r'Scan checked. \((k;k,0,k,0)\) is an immediate counterexample; Claim 2 of the original announcement explicitly lists the family.')
corr('P000316-P000317','Genericity and stability slogans',r'General position has no parameter; every parameter-free indecomposable is stable.',r'Genericity and rigidity depend on the dimension vector and orbit; exceptional forms can deform to parameter families.',r'Four distinct lines already vary in cross ratio. The notebook\'s own displayed \(k=1\) defect-zero exceptional form is a coincident-line limit of distinct-line systems.')
talk(199,'1970-11-02','D. A. Kazhdan','P000317-P000319','A repaired affine proof, a Picard-group exercise, and logarithmic forms',r'The Kazhdan block begins after the four-subspace discussion on P000317. The brief Deligne theorem at the top of P000319 precedes Sinai\'s explicit heading and is recorded here as an unattributed mathematical report, not assigned a new speaker by guesswork.')
item(199,'P000317-P000318','method','Decomposing a Čech cocycle into pieces with a local primitive',r'Write \(m_{ij}=\sum_km_{ij}^{(k)}\), with \(m_{ik}^{(k)}\in M_{f_i}\) and the cocycle identity already valid in \(M_{f_if_j}\).',r'For a finite distinguished-open cover, choose a common \(N\) clearing both denominators and all triple-localization equality errors. Choose \(g_k\in A\) with \(\sum_k g_k f_k^N=1\), and set \(m_{ij}^{(k)}=g_kf_k^N m_{ij}\). Each piece then has the primitive \(n_i^{(k)}=m_{ik}^{(k)}\) for the convention \(m_{ij}=n_i-n_j\). Summing proves the degree-one case of affine acyclicity.',r'This corrects the proof attempt of record 196 and agrees with the localization argument of \citet[Lemma~30.2.1]{stacks2026affine}. The first one-index trial on P000317 has a sign slip; the final P000318 formula has the consistent sign.',refs=['stacks2026affine'],development='annotated')
item(199,'P000318','definition','The sheaf of units is not a quasi-coherent module sheaf',r'\(\mathcal O^*(U)\) consists of functions avoiding all primes of \(U\), and is called a subsheaf of the structure sheaf.',r'\(\mathcal O_X^\times\) is the sheaf of invertible regular functions under multiplication. It is a subsheaf of sets and an abelian sheaf under that law, but not an \(\mathcal O_X\)-submodule: zero is not a unit and units are not closed under addition. Affine acyclicity for quasi-coherent modules therefore does not apply to it.',r'The Picard-group interpretation used in \citet[section~59.69]{stacks2026picardcurves} provides the relevant cohomology. The different algebraic structures are essential to the next exercise.',refs=['stacks2026picardcurves'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
item(199,'P000318','problem','Nonzero first cohomology of units on a punctured elliptic curve',r'For \(A=\mathbf C[x,y]/(y^2-x^3+1)\), prove \(H^1(\operatorname{Spec}A,\mathcal O^*)\ne0\).',r'Let \(E\) be the smooth projective completion of \(y^2=x^3-1\), with point at infinity \(O\), and \(U=E\setminus\{O\}\). Then \(H^1_{\mathrm{Zar}}(U,\mathcal O_U^\times)=\operatorname{Pic}(U)\cong\operatorname{Pic}(E)/\mathbf Z[O]\cong\operatorname{Pic}^0(E)\cong E(\mathbf C)\), which is nonzero.',r'\citet[proof of Lemma~59.69.3]{stacks2026picardcurves} gives the quotient by removed-point classes. Together with the degree decomposition for a pointed elliptic curve, this solves the notebook exercise. No claim that an arbitrary topological torus has this sheaf cohomology is intended.',refs=['stacks2026picardcurves'],problem='GP-1970-24',literal='correct',status='A solved standard exercise; affine quasi-coherent vanishing does not cover the multiplicative unit sheaf.')
item(199,'P000318','theorem','Cohomology of punctured affine space',r'For \(S=\mathbf A^n\setminus\{0\}\), only degrees zero and \(n-1\) can be nonzero, and the latter is infinite-dimensional.',r'Over a field \(k\), for \(n\geq2\), \(H^0(S,\mathcal O)=k[x_1,\ldots,x_n]\), \(H^q(S,\mathcal O)=0\) for \(q\notin\{0,n-1\}\), and \(H^{n-1}\) is freely spanned over \(k\) by monomials \(x_1^{-a_1}\cdots x_n^{-a_n}\), all \(a_i\geq1\). For \(n=1\), \(H^0=k[x,x^{-1}]\) instead.',r'The detailed calculation is carried out in record 201. It is the localization-complex calculation underlying \citet[Lemma~30.8.1]{stacks2026projective}; the affine-cover acyclicity needed for it is \citet[Lemma~30.2.2]{stacks2026affine}.',refs=['stacks2026projective','stacks2026affine'],problem='GP-1970-25',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(199,'P000319','theorem','Deligne\'s logarithmic de Rham description of an open variety',r'A complete smooth \(X\), a transversal codimension-one boundary \(Y\), and local forms \(df/f\) are described; resolution reduces to this situation and the cohomology ring is said to be independent of the resolution.',r'For a smooth complex algebraic variety \(U\), choose a smooth proper compactification \(j:U\hookrightarrow X\) whose reduced boundary \(D=X\setminus U\) is a simple normal-crossings divisor. Then \(\Omega_X^\bullet(\log D)\) computes \(H^*(U^{\mathrm{an}},\mathbf C)\) by hypercohomology, compatibly with products. The resulting cohomology and the mixed Hodge structure do not depend on the chosen such compactification. Smoothness, characteristic zero, and normal crossings are necessary parts of this formulation.',r'\citet[Proposition~3.1.8 and Theorem~3.2.5]{deligne1971hodge} supplies the comparison and independence. The paper was received 15 September 1970 and printed in 1971. The notebook already reports it as Deligne\'s theorem; the submission date is not treated as a public-release date.',refs=['deligne1971hodge'],cls='E',reconstruction=r'Hypercohomology, the smooth open variety, and the precise normal-crossings condition replace the compressed word ring. The mixed-Hodge statement is additional published context, not words attributed to the notebook.',status='Established; the notebook records an already circulating theorem, with the first printing checked here in 1971.',speaker='Unattributed report of P. Deligne\'s theorem')
problem('GP-1970-24','The unit-sheaf cohomology of the punctured elliptic curve',r'For \(A=\mathbf C[x,y]/(y^2-x^3+1)\), show \(H^1(\operatorname{Spec}A,\mathcal O^\times)\ne0\).',['1970-199.3'],r'Solved by the Picard group of a smooth elliptic curve with its point at infinity removed.',['stacks2026picardcurves'])
corr('P000317','Trial primitive for a one-cocycle',r'With \(m_{ij}=n_i-n_j\) and \(n_1=0\), take \(n_i=m_{1i}\).',r'Take \(n_i=-m_{1i}=m_{i1}\).',r'Scan checked. The final P000318 formula \(n_i^{(k)}=m_{ik}^{(k)}\) already has the correct orientation.')

talk(200,'1970-11-02','Ya. G. Sinai','P000319','Powers\' factor criterion and asymptotic independence',r'The whole Sinai block is on P000319 below the separator. Its principal formula has an existential quantifier where a uniform tail-algebra estimate is needed; the original Powers theorem resolves that compression exactly.')
item(200,'P000319','theorem','The uniform relative-commutant criterion for a factor state',r'The displayed theorem says \(\forall x\,\forall\varepsilon>0\,\exists y\) satisfying the correlation estimate, with \(y\) in a local commutant.',r'For a state \(\varphi\) of the spin UHF algebra, \(M_\varphi\) is a factor if and only if for every \(x\in\mathcal A\) and \(\varepsilon>0\) there exists \(n\) such that \[|\varphi(xy)-\varphi(x)\varphi(y)|\leq\varepsilon\|y\|\quad\text{for all }y\in\mathcal A([-n,n])^{\prime}\cap\mathcal A.\] The quantifier is over every element of the tail relative commutant, not one chosen \(y\).',r'\citet[Theorem~2.5 and proof; Russian translation pp.~100--101]{powers1967representations} gives this criterion, after rescaling \(x\) to incorporate \(\varepsilon\). The original theorem is 1967; the Russian translation available to the seminar is 1969.',refs=['powers1967representations'],literal='false as written',development='annotated',problem='GP-1970-22',status='Established with the corrected uniform quantifiers. The literal existential condition is vacuous because \(y=0\) always works.')
item(200,'P000319','construction','The classical tail-independence form of the criterion',r'For measure-type states the estimate becomes \(\sup_B|\mu(A\cap B)-\mu(A)\mu(B)|\to0\).',r'For the product-diagonal states of 1970-195.3, the equivalent classical condition is: for every fixed cylinder event \(A\), \[\sup_{B\in\sigma(\omega_j:|j|>n)}|\mu(A\cap B)-\mu(A)\mu(B)|\longrightarrow0.\] Uniformity in the distant event is required. For a two-sided lattice, outside \([-n,n]\) replaces a one-sided first-\(n\)-coordinates description.',r'This follows by restricting the correlation criterion of \citet[Theorem~2.5]{powers1967representations} to diagonal projections and extending by simple-function approximation. It applies to the genuinely product-diagonal construction, not automatically to the weaker commuting-density definition of record 197.',refs=['powers1967representations'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',problem='GP-1970-22',reconstruction='The two-sided tail sigma-field and the fixed-cylinder quantifier are made explicit; the notebook\'s measure terminology is not used to bypass its diagonalization error.')
item(200,'P000319','theorem','Product states satisfy the factor criterion',r'Product measures with site probabilities bounded below by a fixed \(c>0\) are given as examples.',r'Every infinite product state of the tensor-product matrix algebra is a factor state. The lower bound on site probabilities in the notebook is sufficient but unnecessary; pure site states are allowed as well. For an observable supported in \([-n,n]\), correlation with the outside algebra factorizes exactly, and norm approximation handles quasi-local observables.',r'\citet[Lemma~4.1, Russian translation p.~113]{powers1967representations} proves the product-state assertion. Section~4 distinguishes type I, tracial type \(\mathrm{II}_1\), and nontracial type III examples.',refs=['powers1967representations'],literal='correct',problem='GP-1970-22')
item(200,'P000319','theorem','The finite-temperature one-dimensional Gibbs state is primary',r'The last line says that the state obtained from the Hamiltonian is a factor.',r'For the self-adjoint finite-range one-dimensional interaction and finite-temperature Gibbs limit of 1970-195.4--5, the GNS von Neumann algebra is a factor. This conclusion does not assert that all states of the algebra, or all infinite-volume Gibbs states in higher dimensions, are factors.',r'The primary-state conclusion is explicitly in the abstract of \citet{araki1969gibbs}. The notebook is reporting a known result within the stated spin-chain setup.',refs=['araki1969gibbs'],verification='publisher metadata checked',problem='GP-1970-22')
problem('GP-1970-22','Factor criteria for infinite quantum spin states',r'When does a state of the spin UHF algebra have a factor GNS representation, and which types can occur?',['1970-195.8','1970-197.4','1970-200.1','1970-200.2','1970-200.3','1970-200.4'],r'Powers\' 1967 relative-commutant correlation criterion answers the factor question; product states and Araki\'s one-dimensional finite-range Gibbs states supply established families. The type depends on the state. No identical spin-chain problem was found in the supplied previous index.',['powers1967representations','araki1969gibbs'])
corr('P000319','Powers theorem quantifiers',r'\(\forall x\,\forall\varepsilon>0\,\exists y\) with the estimate.',r'\(\forall x\,\forall\varepsilon>0\,\exists n\,\forall y\in\mathcal A([-n,n])^{\prime}\cap\mathcal A\) with the estimate.',r'The scan visibly writes existential \(y\), making the condition vacuous. Powers Theorem 2.5 and its proof supply the uniform quantifiers.')

talk(201,'1970-11-16','D. A. Kazhdan','P000320-P000323','Punctured affine space, Koszul exactness, and residue duality',r'The manuscript\'s 16/XI date supplies the ISO date missing from the catalog\'s underscore-spelled entry. The computation is valid, but the final module-Hom identification is not: the residue pairing produces a restricted linear dual, not \(\operatorname{Hom}_R(R,k)\).')
item(201,'P000320','construction','The principal-open cover of punctured affine space',r'\(X=\operatorname{Spec}A[x_1,\ldots,x_n]\setminus\{0\}\) is covered by the \(X_i\) where \(x_i\ne0\).',r'Put \(R=A[x_1,\ldots,x_n]\), \(I=(x_1,\ldots,x_n)\), and \(U=\operatorname{Spec}R\setminus V(I)\). Over a general base ring, \(V(I)\cong\operatorname{Spec}A\) is the zero section, not a single point. The cover is \(D(x_i)\), with sections \(R_{x_i}\); every intersection inverts all corresponding variables.',r'The localization cover and its cohomology computation are the same monomial complex used in \citet[Lemma~30.8.1]{stacks2026projective}, justified by \citet[Lemma~30.2.2]{stacks2026affine}.',refs=['stacks2026projective','stacks2026affine'],literal='correct')
item(201,'P000320','theorem','Global functions extend across the zero section in dimension at least two',r'The notes identify \(H^0\) with the polynomial ring and explicitly exclude \(n=1\) in the preliminary calculation.',r'For every commutative ring \(A\) and \(n\geq2\), \(\Gamma(U,\mathcal O_U)=A[x_1,\ldots,x_n]\). For \(n=1\), \(U=D(x_1)\) and the answer is \(A[x_1,x_1^{-1}]\). The polynomial-extension statement follows by intersecting the relevant Laurent subrings, or by monomial Čech exactness.',r'The affine-cover proof is classical; the graded version is computed in \citet[proof of Lemma~30.8.1]{stacks2026projective}. This continues the earlier punctured-affine examples listed as 1965-58.7 and affine-cover methods 1965-70.6.',refs=['stacks2026projective'],literal='correct',problem='GP-1970-25')
item(201,'P000321','method','The three-variable one-cocycle calculation is a Koszul syzygy',r'The \(n=3\) calculation clears denominators and separates polynomial multiples of powers of the coordinates.',r'The sequence \(x_1^N,\ldots,x_n^N\) is regular in \(A[x_1,\ldots,x_n]\) for every \(N\geq1\), even when \(A\) has zero divisors. Its Koszul complex is exact away from degree zero. Thus a relation among the powered coordinates is generated by the elementary skew relations; after localization this gives the required Čech primitive. In three variables it proves \(H^1(U,\mathcal O)=0\).',r'The notebook announces the Koszul complex as the next language for this calculation. The monomial proof in \citet[proof of Lemma~30.8.1]{stacks2026projective} verifies the same vanishing without depending on the inconsistent intermediate polynomial subscripts.',refs=['stacks2026projective'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',reconstruction='The regular-sequence statement is supplied as a precise modern formulation of the polynomial-divisibility argument.')
item(201,'P000321-P000322','theorem','Vanishing in all intermediate degrees',r'The argument for \(H^1\) is extended to \(H^q\), except the last possible degree.',r'For \(n\geq2\), \(H^q(U,\mathcal O_U)=0\) when \(1\leq q\leq n-2\); it is also zero for \(q\geq n\) because the cover has \(n\) members. Splitting the Čech complex by Laurent monomials gives a simplex complex unless every coordinate has negative exponent, which contributes only in degree \(n-1\).',r'This is the ungraded version of \citet[proof of Lemma~30.8.1]{stacks2026projective}. The complete list of nonzero degrees agrees with the preliminary announcement in record 199.',refs=['stacks2026projective'],literal='correct',problem='GP-1970-25')
item(201,'P000322-P000323','theorem','The top cohomology as negative Laurent monomials',r'Top cohomology consists of negative powers of every variable; multiplication by polynomials is followed by throwing away terms with nonnegative powers.',r'For \(n\geq2\), \[H^{n-1}(U,\mathcal O_U)\cong R_{x_1\cdots x_n}\big/\sum_iR_{x_1\cdots\widehat{x_i}\cdots x_n}\cong\bigoplus_{a_1,\ldots,a_n\geq1}A\,x_1^{-a_1}\cdots x_n^{-a_n}.\] The quotient, not ordinary Laurent multiplication alone, determines its \(R\)-module action.',r'\citet[Lemma~30.8.1 and its monomial proof]{stacks2026projective} gives the homogeneous pieces of this module. Over a nonzero field it is infinite-dimensional. The case \(n=1\) belongs to degree-zero global sections and must be kept separate.',refs=['stacks2026projective'],literal='correct',problem='GP-1970-25')
item(201,'P000323','construction','Residue pairing with the restricted linear dual',r'\(H^{n-1}=\operatorname{Hom}_{R}(R,k)\) is written twice, with \(k\) the evaluation-at-zero module; the proposed pairing takes the coefficient of \((x_1\cdots x_n)^{-1}\).',r'Over a field \(k\), the pairing \(\langle f,\eta\rangle=\operatorname{coeff}_{x_1^{-1}\cdots x_n^{-1}}(f\eta)\) is nondegenerate and identifies top cohomology with the finite-support, or graded restricted, \(k\)-linear dual \(R^\circ=\bigoplus_{d\geq0}\operatorname{Hom}_k(R_d,k)\), with the grading shifted by \(-n\). Its \(R\)-action is \((r\ell)(f)=\ell(rf)\). It is neither \(\operatorname{Hom}_R(R,k)\cong k\) nor the full algebraic dual \(\prod_d\operatorname{Hom}_k(R_d,k)\).',r'The monomial bases in \citet[Lemma~30.8.1]{stacks2026projective} verify the pairing term by term. The source\'s reference to Serre duality is a thematic link: this affine local-duality calculation needs the restricted dual and the differential/degree convention stated here.',refs=['stacks2026projective','serre1955coherent'],literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated',problem='GP-1970-25',status='The corrected residue-dual identification is established. The displayed \(R\)-linear Hom has the wrong dimension and action.')
item(201,'P000323','remark','General base-ring version and the limits of the duality slogan',r'The theorem is restated with coefficient ring \(A\), writing \(H^{n-1}=\operatorname{Hom}_{H^0}(H^0,A)\).',r'The Laurent-quotient and direct-sum description in 1970-201.5 works for every commutative \(A\). Each homogeneous polynomial piece \(R_d\) is finite free, so coefficient extraction gives the corresponding restricted \(A\)-linear dual. The general-base statement does not turn this into an \(R\)-linear Hom to the evaluation-at-zero module.',r'The arbitrary-ring computation is explicitly allowed in \citet[Lemma~30.8.1]{stacks2026projective}. No field-only dimension argument is used to justify extension of the Laurent formula to general \(A\).',refs=['stacks2026projective'],literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated')
item(201,'P000322-P000323','remark','Commuting operators and the announced Koszul complex',r'The notes briefly connect the calculation with several commuting operators and promise the Koszul complex next time.',r'Commuting operators give a module over a polynomial ring, and their Koszul complex is a natural multivariable replacement for a single operator complex. The manuscript gives no precise spectral set, resolvent theorem, or functional calculus to identify beyond that construction.',r'The polynomial-module/Koszul connection is precise, but no exact additional operator theorem was verified from the abbreviated remark. The item is retained as a methodological indication rather than expanded into an unrecorded multivariable spectral theorem.',refs=[],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='medium',status='The exact intended operator-theoretic statement remains unspecified; no open problem is inferred.')
problem('GP-1970-25','Punctured affine cohomology and residue duality',r'Compute \(H^*(\mathbf A^n\setminus\{0\},\mathcal O)\) and identify the module and residue pairing in its top nonzero degree.',['1970-199.4','1970-201.2','1970-201.4','1970-201.5','1970-201.6'],r'Solved classical localization/Koszul calculation. For \(n\geq2\) the top group is the module of Laurent monomials negative in every variable; its residue dual is restricted linear, not ring-linear. Earlier affine-cover and non-affineness entries 1965-70.6 and 1965-58.7 are related background, not an identical unresolved question.',['stacks2026projective','stacks2026affine','serre1955coherent'])
corr('P000320','Intersection-localization display',r'The list of inverted variables starts after the first indicated index.',r'Invert every variable indexing the intersection, including \(x_{k_0}\).',r'By the preceding definition the intersection is \(D(x_{k_0}\cdots x_{k_q})\); omission would describe a different open set.')
corr('P000323','Top-degree module dual',r'\(\operatorname{Hom}_{R}(R,k)\), or \(\operatorname{Hom}_{H^0}(H^0,A)\).',r'The graded restricted linear dual over \(k\), respectively \(A\), with contragredient \(R\)-action and degree shift.',r'Scan checked: the ring subscript and evaluation-at-zero explanation are explicit. Ring-linear Hom is one-dimensional over \(k\), whereas the preceding negative-monomial module is infinite-dimensional.')
corr('P000320','General-base zero notation',r'The notation \(\{0\}\) denotes the ideal \((x_1,\ldots,x_n)\) and, explicitly, all prime ideals containing it.',r'In modern notation the removed closed subscheme is the zero section \(V(x_1,\ldots,x_n)\cong\operatorname{Spec}A\).',r'The Russian text and scan P320 explicitly include all containing primes and distinguish the field case. This is a clarification of notation already correctly explained in the source, not a mathematical error.',typ='ambiguity')
corr('P000320','Catalog date of record 201','16_Nov-70; no ISO date.','1970-11-16.',r'The manuscript header clearly reads 16/XI; the catalog\'s underscore spelling prevented its supplied ISO rendering.',typ='provenance')

bib('arens1963invertible','article',dict(author='Arens, Richard',title='The group of invertible elements of a commutative Banach algebra',journal='Studia Mathematica, Special Series',volume=1,pages='21--23',year=1963,doi='10.4064/sm-SpecialSeries-1-21-23'),'https://www.impan.pl/en/publishing-house/journals-and-series/studia-mathematica/all/all/51/2/100340/en/publishing-house/journals-and-series/studia-mathematica/all//1/95297/the-group-of-invertible-elements-of-a-commutative-banach-algebra','Publisher metadata opened; PDF access returned 403. The result was read in the introduction of Lupton--Phillips--Schochet--Smith, arXiv:math/0509269, where the Arens--Royden isomorphism is explicitly stated.')
bib('luptonphillipsschochetsmith2007banach','misc',dict(author='Lupton, Gregory and Phillips, N. Christopher and Schochet, Claude L. and Smith, Samuel B.',title='Banach algebras and rational homotopy theory',year=2007,eprint='math/0509269',archivePrefix='arXiv',howpublished='Author preprint, version 2, 27 April 2007'),'https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0509269','Full author preprint opened. Introduction, manuscript p. 2, explicitly states the Arens--Royden theorem for the invertible group and H1 of the maximal ideal space; the present citation uses that stated theorem, not the later rational-homotopy results.')
bib('gohberg1967toeplitz','article',dict(author='Gohberg, Israel C.',title='On Toeplitz matrices composed of the Fourier coefficients of piecewise continuous functions',journal='Functional Analysis and Its Applications',volume=1,number=2,pages='166--167',year=1967,doi='10.1007/BF01076090'),'https://www.mathnet.ru/eng/faa2821','Publisher metadata and full original Russian article, Funktsionalnyi Analiz i ego Prilozheniya 1(2), 91--92, opened. Theorem 1 on p. 91 gives the completed-curve criterion and the separate kernel/cokernel dimensions, hence negative winding for the conventional Fredholm index. Received 5 November 1966.')
bib('gohbergkrupnik1970algebra','article',dict(author='Gohberg, Israel C. and Krupnik, Naum Ya.',title='The algebra generated by one-dimensional singular integral operators with piecewise continuous coefficients',journal='Functional Analysis and Its Applications',volume=4,number=3,pages='193--201',year=1970,doi='10.1007/BF01075239'),'https://www.mathnet.ru/eng/faa2663','Publisher metadata and full Russian original, 4(3), 26--36, opened. Theorem 0.2, Theorem 6.1 on the quotient symbol algebra, and Theorem 7.1 on the negative-winding index were read. Received 5 June 1969; printed 1970. This is a related singular-integral algebra framework; Gohberg 1967 is the exact scalar Toeplitz theorem.')
bib('stacks2026projconstruction','misc',dict(author='{The Stacks Project Authors}',title='Proj, projective space, and hyperplane sections',year=2026,howpublished='The Stacks Project, Tags 01M3, 01NA, and 089X'),'https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01M3','Sections 27.8 and 33.35, and Lemma 27.12.3 opened. The degree-zero localization construction, quotient-line-bundle interpretation of Proj, and Lemma 33.35.3 requiring a general hyperplane over an infinite field were read.')

talk(202,'1970-11-23','I. M. Gelfand','P000324','Plans for the next semester',r'The plan names Arnold and Gabrielov on Griffiths and integrals of rational differential forms over cycles; S. Gelfand on Brauer theory; Sinai on factors; Knorr on a new proof of a theorem of Grauert; and new work of Atiyah on skew Fredholm operators, alongside Gohberg, Krupnik, and Gorin. These are proposed subjects, with no precise new theorem asserted. The catalog dates this record 23 November, whereas the clearly visible manuscript header reads 22/XI. The isolated Arnold heading on P000325, about vector fields in general position, supplies no mathematical statement and is preserved here as an uncatalogued heading between Gorin and Kazhdan.',kind='plan')
corr('P000324','Date of the program','Catalog record 202: 23-Nov-70; manuscript 22/XI.','Retain the catalog date as catalog provenance and explicitly report the manuscript date 22 November.','Scan checked: both digits of 22 are visible. The surviving page does not determine whether the plan was written the previous day or the catalog date is mistaken.',typ='provenance')

talk(203,'1970-11-23','E. A. Gorin','P000324-P000325','Symbol algebras, the Arens–Royden index, and Toeplitz operators',r'The Gorin block begins at the boxed name in the lower part of P000324 and ends before the isolated Arnold heading on P000325. It passes from a quotient by characters to a group-valued index, then gives the completed-curve Fredholm criterion for piecewise continuous Toeplitz symbols. These are reports on established Banach-algebra and Gohberg--Krupnik results; the manuscript does not identify a separate new Gorin theorem.')
item(203,'P000324','construction','The character quotient as a commutative symbol algebra',r'For a noncommutative Banach algebra \(\alpha\), set \(J=\bigcap_\varphi\ker\varphi\), with \(\varphi:\alpha\to\mathbf C\), and call \(A=\alpha/J\) the symbol algebra.',r'Let \(\alpha\) be a unital complex Banach algebra and take all nonzero complex algebra characters. Their kernels are closed, contain every commutator, and have intersection \(J\). If there are characters, \(A=\alpha/J\) is a unital commutative semisimple Banach algebra; if there are none, \(J=\alpha\). For noncommutative \(\alpha\), this character kernel ideal need not be its Jacobson radical.',r'This is the classical character-quotient construction underlying the application of \citet{arens1963invertible}. The related operator symbol algebra is described concretely in \citet[Theorem~6.1]{gohbergkrupnik1970algebra}; no claim of an exact publication of this introductory formulation by Gorin is made.',refs=['arens1963invertible','gohbergkrupnik1970algebra'],match='partial',verification='read the result',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(203,'P000324','remark','The algebra of all bounded Hilbert-space operators has no scalar characters',r'The example says that for the algebra of all bounded operators, \(J=\alpha\): there are no symbols.',r'For a complex Hilbert space \(H\) with \(\dim H\geq2\), \(B(H)\) has no nonzero scalar character, so its character quotient is zero. The dimension-one exception is \(B(\mathbf C)=\mathbf C\). An arbitrary Banach space is not silently included in this Hilbert-space assertion.',r'The claim is an elementary obstruction to this particular symbol construction. In finite dimension matrix units exclude characters; in infinite Hilbert dimension two orthogonal isometries do so. The Fredholm quotient by compact operators is a different construction, and is the operator-algebra context of \citet{gohbergkrupnik1970algebra}.',refs=['gohbergkrupnik1970algebra'],match='thematic only',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The Hilbert-space interpretation and dimension restriction are supplied; the source does not specify the underlying space.')
item(203,'P000324','theorem','The group-valued Arens–Royden index',r'\(\mathcal E(A)\) is the invertible group, \(\mathcal E_1(A)\) its identity component, and \(\operatorname{ind}T\in\mathcal E(A)/\mathcal E_1(A)=H^1(\mathfrak M_A,\mathbf Z)\).',r'For a unital commutative complex Banach algebra \(A\), the Gelfand transform induces \(A^\times/\exp(A)\cong\check H^1(\mathfrak M_A;\mathbf Z)\), and \(\exp(A)\) is the identity component of \(A^\times\). If \(\psi:\alpha\to A\) and \(\psi(T)\) is invertible, its component class is a group-valued index. It need not be a numerical Fredholm index unless the operator extension and its connecting map are specified.',r'The result is the Arens--Royden theorem, predating the seminar: \citet{arens1963invertible}, and the explicit modern formulation in \citet[introduction, p.~2]{luptonphillipsschochetsmith2007banach}. The notebook itself correctly stresses that the index need not be a number.',refs=['arens1963invertible','luptonphillipsschochetsmith2007banach'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',status='Established; Čech cohomology is the natural theory for a general compact maximal ideal space.')
item(203,'P000325','construction','Hardy-space Toeplitz operators with piecewise continuous symbols',r'\(T_af=(af)_+\) acts on \(H_2(\Delta)\), the holomorphic functions with square-summable Taylor coefficients; \(\alpha\) is generated by these operators.',r'Identify \(H^2(\mathbb D)\) with the nonnegative-Fourier subspace of \(L^2(S^1)\), and let \(P_+\) be the orthogonal projection. For \(a\in L^\infty(S^1)\), \(T_a=P_+M_a|_{H^2}\) is bounded with \(\|T_a\|\leq\|a\|_\infty\). The example restricts \(a\) to scalar piecewise continuous symbols and considers the norm-closed algebra generated by them; the set of individual \(T_a\) is not itself generally closed under multiplication.',r'This is the Toeplitz matrix setup of \citet[p.~91]{gohberg1967toeplitz}. The related quotient-symbol construction is developed in \citet{gohbergkrupnik1970algebra}.',refs=['gohberg1967toeplitz','gohbergkrupnik1970algebra'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(203,'P000325','theorem','The completed-symbol curve and the Fredholm index',r'At every discontinuity join the two limiting symbol values by a segment; \(T_a\) is Fredholm exactly when the completed curve avoids zero, and its index is the number of turns.',r'For a scalar piecewise continuous symbol on the positively oriented circle with finitely many jumps, form the oriented closed curve \(a_2\) by inserting a straight segment from each left limit to its right limit. On \(H^2\), \(T_a\) is Fredholm iff \(0\notin a_2\). With \(\operatorname{ind}T_a=\dim\ker T_a-\dim\operatorname{coker}T_a\), \(\operatorname{ind}T_a=-\operatorname{wind}(a_2,0)\). The straight-segment completion is specific to this Hilbert-space exponent.',r'Exact match with \citet[Theorem~1, p.~91]{gohberg1967toeplitz}, received 5 November 1966 and printed 1967. Its separate kernel and cokernel formulas fix the sign. The later singular-integral algebra treatment is \citet[Theorem~7.1]{gohbergkrupnik1970algebra}, received 5 June 1969 and printed 1970.',refs=['gohberg1967toeplitz','gohbergkrupnik1970algebra'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated',status='Established before the lecture. The notebook leaves the winding orientation and Fredholm-index sign convention unstated.')
corr('P000325','Toeplitz index sign','The index is the number of turns.',r'For positive circle orientation and \(\operatorname{ind}=\dim\ker-\dim\operatorname{coker}\), use minus the winding number.',r'The convention is unstated in the source; \(T_z\) is the unilateral shift with index \(-1\). Gohberg 1967, Theorem 1, gives the same sign.',typ='ambiguity')

talk(204,'1970-11-23','D. A. Kazhdan','P000325-P000328','Projective space, Serre twists, and coherent cohomology',r'Kazhdan constructs projective space and its twisting sheaves, states global generation, cohomological finiteness and vanishing, and proves polynomiality of Euler characteristics. The later proofs require a Noetherian base for coherence and finite generation; the Euler characteristic is then specialized to a field. The hyperplane argument needs a section that is a non-zero-divisor on the sheaf.')
item(204,'P000325','construction','Projective space as the scalar quotient of the punctured affine space',r'\(A^*\) acts on \(A^{n+1}\setminus\{0\}\), and \(A^*=\operatorname{Spec}A[x,y]/(xy-1)\).',r'Over a ring \(A\), the multiplicative group scheme is \(\mathbf G_{m,A}=\operatorname{Spec}A[t,t^{-1}]\). It acts by simultaneous scalar multiplication on \(U=\mathbf A_A^{n+1}\setminus V(x_0,\ldots,x_n)\); the scheme quotient is \(\mathbf P_A^n\), with \(U\to\mathbf P_A^n\) the associated \(\mathbf G_m\)-torsor. A quotient of sets of \(A\)-valued points is not generally a substitute for this scheme quotient.',r'This is the standard projective-space construction, whose functorial line-bundle interpretation is \citet[Lemma~27.12.3]{stacks2026projconstruction}. It is expository material in the lecture.',refs=['stacks2026projconstruction'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
item(204,'P000325','definition','Homogeneous primes and the degree-zero localization sheaf',r'Projective points are called homogeneous prime ideals not contained in \((x_0,\ldots,x_n)\); sections on \(P_f\) are \((A[x_0,\ldots,x_n]_f)_0\).',r'Let \(S=A[x_0,\ldots,x_n]\) be standard graded. Then \(\operatorname{Proj}S\) consists of homogeneous prime ideals \(\mathfrak p\) such that \(S_+\not\subseteq\mathfrak p\). Its basic open \(D_+(f)\), for homogeneous positive-degree \(f\), has coordinate ring \((S_f)_0\). The irrelevant ideal must not be contained in the prime; the source reverses this containment.',r'The construction and structure sheaf are given in \citet[section~27.8]{stacks2026projconstruction}. The formula for the sections is correct after fixing the prime-ideal condition.',refs=['stacks2026projconstruction'],literal='false as written',development='annotated')
item(204,'P000326','theorem','Affine charts on projective space',r'\(P_{x_i}\cong A^n\), denoted \(U_i\).',r'The chart \(D_+(x_i)\) is \(\operatorname{Spec}A[x_0/x_i,\ldots,\widehat{x_i/x_i},\ldots,x_n/x_i]\cong\mathbf A_A^n\). These \(n+1\) charts cover \(\mathbf P_A^n\); on overlaps the coordinate ratios give the gluing maps.',r'This follows directly from degree-zero localization in \citet[section~27.8]{stacks2026projconstruction}; no hypothesis on \(A\) beyond commutativity is needed.',refs=['stacks2026projconstruction'],literal='correct')
item(204,'P000326','definition','Twisting sheaves and the Hopf-bundle convention',r'\(\Gamma(P_f,\mathcal O(k))=(S_f)_k\), \(\mathcal O(1)\) is called the Hopf sheaf, and \(\mathcal F(k)=\mathcal F\otimes\mathcal O(k)\).',r'The invertible sheaf \(\mathcal O(k)=\widetilde{S(k)}\) has the specified degree-\(k\) sections on basic opens. On complex projective space, the tautological line bundle is \(\mathcal O(-1)\); \(\mathcal O(1)\) is its dual. Since Hopf bundle conventions differ, the degree convention rather than that name fixes the meaning. Tensoring by \(\mathcal O(k)\) is exact.',r'The standard graded construction is compatible with \citet[Lemma~27.12.3]{stacks2026projconstruction} and the cohomology convention of \citet[Lemma~30.8.1]{stacks2026projective}.',refs=['stacks2026projconstruction','stacks2026projective'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
item(204,'P000326','definition','Coherence and finite presentations',r'A coherent sheaf is described locally by \(\mathcal O^J\to\mathcal O^I\to\mathcal F\to0\), with \(I,J\) finite.',r'On a locally Noetherian scheme, coherence is equivalent to local finite presentation, and kernels of maps between coherent sheaves are coherent. For the following Serre finiteness argument assume \(A\) Noetherian. Over arbitrary noncoherent rings, merely writing a finite presentation is not the general definition of a coherent module.',r'The Noetherian hypothesis is explicit in \citet[Lemma~30.14.1]{stacks2026projective}. The classical formulation in \citet{serre1955coherent} works in a Noetherian algebraic setting.',refs=['stacks2026projective','serre1955coherent'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated')
item(204,'P000326-P000327','theorem','Finiteness and cohomological dimension of coherent sheaves',r'All \(H^i(P,\mathcal F)\) are finite-type \(A\)-modules, and they vanish for \(i>n\).',r'If \(A\) is Noetherian and \(\mathcal F\) coherent on \(\mathbf P_A^n\), every \(H^i(\mathbf P_A^n,\mathcal F)\) is a finite \(A\)-module. The vanishing for \(i>n\) follows from the standard affine cover and also holds for arbitrary quasi-coherent sheaves over an arbitrary base ring.',r'These are the projective finiteness and dimension statements in \citet{serre1955coherent}; their precise ring hypotheses and descending-induction proof are \citet[Lemma~30.14.1]{stacks2026projective}.',refs=['serre1955coherent','stacks2026projective'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(204,'P000326-P000328','theorem','Serre vanishing for large positive twists',r'For sufficiently large \(k\), \(H^i(P,\mathcal F(k))=0\) for \(i\ne0\).',r'For \(A\) Noetherian and \(\mathcal F\) coherent on \(\mathbf P_A^n\), there is \(k_0\) such that all \(H^i(\mathbf P_A^n,\mathcal F(k))\) vanish for \(i>0\) and \(k\geq k_0\). The bound can be chosen simultaneously for the finitely many relevant cohomological degrees.',r'Classical Serre vanishing, \citet{serre1955coherent}; \citet[Lemma~30.14.1]{stacks2026projective} verifies this exact formulation. It was established long before the lecture.',refs=['serre1955coherent','stacks2026projective'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(204,'P000326-P000327','theorem','Global generation after twisting',r'The main lemma says that \(\mathcal F(k)\) is generated by global sections for every sufficiently large \(k\), hence \(\mathcal O^N\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal F(k)\).',r'In the same Noetherian coherent setting, \(\mathcal F(k)\) is generated by finitely many global sections for all \(k\gg0\). Equivalently, for some \(m,N\) there is a surjection \(\mathcal O(-m)^N\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal F\); larger twists preserve global generation because \(\mathcal O(1)\) is globally generated.',r'The finite sum of twists surjecting onto \(\mathcal F\) is \citet[Lemma~30.14.1(1)]{stacks2026projective}. This is Serre\'s standard projective construction, not a new seminar assertion.',refs=['stacks2026projective','serre1955coherent'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(204,'P000326-P000327','method','Clearing a section’s poles along a coordinate hyperplane',r'A section \(\alpha\) over \(P_{x_0}\) is said to extend after changing the twist; multiplication by \(x_0\) clears its pole.',r'Given a local section of a quasi-coherent finite-type sheaf over \(D_+(x_0)\), some power of the coordinate section \(x_0\) turns it into the restriction of a global section of an appropriate positive twist. Applied to finitely many local generators on the finitely many standard charts, a common power yields the global-generation theorem.',r'The extension-and-clearing step is the standard projective argument used in \citet{serre1955coherent} and in the proof underlying \citet[Lemma~30.14.1(1)]{stacks2026projective}. The word poles is a useful picture, but for a general coherent sheaf the rigorous operation is multiplication and localization.',refs=['serre1955coherent','stacks2026projective'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
item(204,'P000327','theorem','Cohomology of the twists from the graded punctured-affine complex',r'\(\bigoplus_{k\in\mathbf Z}H^i(\mathcal O(k))\) is identified with \(H^i(\mathbf A^{n+1}\setminus\{0\},\mathcal O)\).',r'For \(n\geq1\) and every ring \(A\), \(H^0(\mathbf P_A^n,\mathcal O(k))=S_k\) for \(k\geq0\), while \(H^n\) is freely generated over \(A\) by monomials \(x_0^{-a_0}\cdots x_n^{-a_n}\) with every \(a_i\geq1\) and \(-\sum a_i=k\). All other groups vanish. These are precisely the degree-\(k\) pieces of the punctured-affine Čech complex from record 201. For \(n=0\), \(\mathcal O(k)\) is trivial and has \(H^0=A\) for all \(k\).',r'The arbitrary-ring monomial computation is \citet[Lemma~30.8.1]{stacks2026projective}; the notebook correctly clarifies that its sum sign means direct sum.',refs=['stacks2026projective'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(204,'P000327-P000328','method','Descending induction through a coherent kernel',r'Use \(0\to\mathcal G\to\mathcal O(-m)^N\to\mathcal F\to0\), and induct downwards in cohomological degree for finiteness and vanishing together.',r'The kernel \(\mathcal G\) is coherent over a Noetherian base. The long exact sequence bounds \(H^i(\mathcal F)\) between a quotient of the known twist cohomology and a submodule of \(H^{i+1}(\mathcal G)\). Noetherianity ensures that the latter submodule is finite. After a sufficiently large common twist, both outer terms vanish for \(i>0\).',r'The proof is the one in \citet[proof of Lemma~30.14.1]{stacks2026projective}, and is already in the classical Serre theory. The twist variable must remain the same in all three terms on P000328.',refs=['stacks2026projective','serre1955coherent'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(204,'P000326; P000328','theorem','The Euler characteristic is the Hilbert polynomial',r'Over a field, \(\chi(\mathcal F(k))=\sum_i(-1)^i\dim H^i(\mathcal F(k))\) is polynomial in \(k\); for large \(k\), it equals \(\dim H^0\).',r'For a coherent sheaf \(\mathcal F\) on \(\mathbf P_K^n\) over a field, the integer-valued function \(k\mapsto\chi(\mathcal F(k))\), for all integers \(k\), is given by a rational polynomial of degree at most \(\dim\operatorname{Supp}\mathcal F\). For \(k\gg0\), higher cohomology vanishes and this polynomial equals \(\dim_K H^0(\mathcal F(k))\).',r'This is the classical Euler/Hilbert polynomial consequence of \citet{serre1955coherent}. The source explicitly restricts to a field before defining the dimension-valued Euler characteristic.',refs=['serre1955coherent'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(204,'P000328','method','Hyperplane induction requires an injective multiplication map',r'The notes use \(0\to\mathcal F\xrightarrow{x_i}\mathcal F(1)\to\overline{\mathcal F}\to0\), then finite differences of Euler characteristics.',r'Over an infinite field, choose a linear form \(\ell\) avoiding the associated points of \(\mathcal F\). Multiplication by \(\ell\) is then injective and its cokernel is supported on the hyperplane. Additivity gives \(\chi(\mathcal F(k+1))-\chi(\mathcal F(k))=\chi(\overline{\mathcal F}(k))\), allowing induction on support dimension. A fixed coordinate \(x_i\) may annihilate a nonzero sheaf and need not be injective. Polynomiality over finite fields follows after a field extension, or by a graded resolution.',r'\citet[Lemma~33.35.3 and Remark~33.35.5]{stacks2026projconstruction} gives the general-hyperplane condition explicitly. This supplies the missing hypothesis in the abbreviated proof, while the intended Hilbert-polynomial conclusion remains classical.',refs=['stacks2026projconstruction','serre1955coherent'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated')
corr('P000325','Primes defining projective space',r'Homogeneous prime ideals not contained in \((x_0,\ldots,x_n)\).',r'Homogeneous prime ideals not containing \((x_0,\ldots,x_n)\).',r'Both transcriptions and the scan preserve the reversed inclusion; \(\operatorname{Proj}\) excludes primes containing the irrelevant ideal.')
corr('P000326-P000328','Scope of coherence and Serre finiteness','The coefficient ring A is not restricted in the displayed theorem.','Assume A Noetherian for the stated coherence and finite-generation proof.','The proof uses coherent kernels and finite generation of submodules of finite modules, which are justified in the standard Noetherian formulation.')
corr('P000326','Hopf sheaf convention',r'\(\mathcal O(1)\) is the Hopf sheaf.',r'With the displayed grading, \(\mathcal O(1)\) is the dual of the tautological \(\mathcal O(-1)\).','The name Hopf is convention-dependent; the section-degree convention is unambiguous.',typ='ambiguity')
corr('P000328','Twisted middle cohomology term',r'\(H^{i_0}(\mathcal F(k))\) between terms twisted by \(k^{\prime}\).',r'\(H^{i_0}(\mathcal F(k^{\prime}))\).','The preceding short exact sequence twists every term by the same new parameter; the scan lacks a clear prime in the middle term.',typ='ambiguity')
corr('P000328','Hyperplane injection',r'An arbitrary coordinate multiplication \(\mathcal F\to\mathcal F(1)\) is injective.',r'Choose a linear form regular on the sheaf, for example a general form over an infinite field.',r'A sheaf supported on \(x_i=0\) is annihilated by \(x_i\); Stacks Lemma 33.35.3 gives the missing avoidance condition.')
corr('P000328','Euler finite-difference equation',r'\(\chi(\mathcal F(k+1))-\chi(\mathcal F(k))=\overline{\mathcal F(k)}\).',r'Put \(\chi(\overline{\mathcal F}(k))\) on the right.',r'Scan checked: no \(\chi\) is visible on the right. The equality is between integers and uses additivity of Euler characteristic.')

talk(205,'','', 'P000329','1971 calendar-year heading',r'The catalog inserts a calendar-year heading before the first February 1971 entries. It is an organizational marker, with no independent mathematical statement. The next surviving dated block on P000329 reads 8/II--71; it remains in the 1970--71 academic-year chapter.',kind='heading')

