bib('simon1970coupling','article',dict(author='Simon, Barry',title='Coupling constant analyticity for the anharmonic oscillator',journal='Annals of Physics',volume=58,pages='76--136',year=1970,doi='10.1016/0003-4916(70)90240-X'),'https://math.caltech.edu/simon/biblio.html','Author bibliography opened, entry 7, confirming title, journal, year, pages and appendix by A. Dicke. Publisher search text describes rigorous results and global three-sheeted monodromy, but opening the publisher full record/PDF returned 403; result verification is therefore indirect, not a full-paper reading.')

talk(206,'1971-02-08','I. M. Gelfand','P000329-P000330','Bott’s integrability obstruction and analytic continuation programs',r'The dated Gelfand passage reports Bott\'s obstruction to deforming a distribution to a foliation, explains the easier submersion case, and lists proposed Sinai, Gabrielov, and Bernstein subjects. The quartic-oscillator note at the top of P000330 precedes Fedosov\'s clear heading; its speaker name is cancelled and uncertain, so it is retained here as an unattributed adjacent remark rather than assigned to Kazhdan from the catalog alone.')
item(206,'P000329','question','Can every tangent distribution be deformed to an integrable one?',r'A tangent subbundle is given, and the notes ask whether an integrable foliation homotopic to it exists; Bott is said to give a negative example.',r'For a smooth constant-rank subbundle \(E\subset TM\), ask whether it can be joined through subbundles of the same rank to the tangent bundle of a nonsingular smooth foliation. In general the answer is negative: the normal bundle of a foliation satisfies characteristic-class restrictions that a general subbundle quotient need not satisfy. Homotopy of subbundles preserves these normal characteristic classes.',r'The reference to Global Analysis, volume 16, identifies \citet{bott1970integrability}, printed in 1970 in proceedings of the July 1968 symposium. Bott\'s later primary exposition explicitly derives the obstruction from characteristic classes in \citet[sections~2 and 4]{bott1976characteristic}. The notebook is reporting this existing obstruction, not posing a still-open universal existence conjecture.',refs=['bott1970integrability','bott1976characteristic'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',problem='GP-1970-26',status='The unrestricted question has a negative answer; additional geometric and homotopy hypotheses lead to more refined existence problems not specified here.')
item(206,'P000329','theorem','Bott vanishing concerns the normal Pontryagin ring',r'Pontryagin classes and their products vanish for \(k>2\dim\) of the indicated bundle, whose identity and degree convention are compressed.',r'Let \(E\subset TM\) be the tangent bundle of a smooth foliation of real codimension \(q\), and let \(\nu=TM/E\). Every homogeneous element of the Pontryagin subring of \(H^*(M;\mathbf R)\) generated by \(p_i(\nu)\) vanishes in cohomological degree \(>2q\). Thus a monomial \(\prod_i p_i(\nu)^{a_i}\) vanishes when \(4\sum_i ia_i>2q\). The assertion concerns the normal bundle, not the tangent bundle of the leaves, and does not assert integral torsion vanishing.',r'\citet[pp.~125--126 and 135--136]{bott1976characteristic} identifies the normal characteristic homomorphism and the truncation giving this precise bound. The historical source is \citet{bott1970integrability}; its original chapter was checked bibliographically, not read in full.',refs=['bott1970integrability','bott1976characteristic'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated',problem='GP-1970-26')
item(206,'P000329','claim','The compressed CP² example cannot be reconstructed as stated',r'The next line says that an example already occurs on \(\mathbf CP^2\), using Pontryagin classes.',r'No rank, normal bundle, or ambient total-space construction is supplied. A literal proper nonsingular real distribution on \(\mathbf CP^2\) is problematic: ranks one and three would force a nonzero vector field despite \(\chi(\mathbf CP^2)=3\); a rank-two splitting would require integers \(a,b\) with \(ab=3\) and \(a^2+b^2=3\), from Euler and first Pontryagin classes, which is impossible. Thus this line cannot identify the asserted homotopy counterexample on that manifold without additional construction or an emendation.',r'The general Bott obstruction is securely identified by \citet{bott1970integrability} and \citet[section~4]{bott1976characteristic}; those checks do not identify the manuscript\'s exact abbreviated projective-space example. It is kept separate from the valid theorem rather than silently replaced by an example on a different space.',refs=['bott1970integrability','bott1976characteristic'],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='medium',development='annotated',reconstruction='The elementary characteristic-class consistency check explains why the literal manifold name is insufficient; no missing example is invented.',status='The exact example intended by the notebook is unidentified. This is an archival identification limit, not a declaration that the general obstruction problem remains open.')
item(206,'P000329','method','The stronger dimension bound for a foliation by submersion fibres',r'For \(\pi:Y\to X\), the notes write \(0\to E\to TY\to\pi^*TX\to0\) and use \(H^k(X)=0\) above \(\dim X\).',r'If \(\pi:Y\to X\) is a smooth submersion with \(E=\ker d\pi\), then \(TY/E\cong\pi^*TX\). Every normal Pontryagin polynomial is pulled back from \(X\), and hence vanishes in cohomological degree \(>\dim_{\mathbf R}X\). A general foliation need not possess a manifold leaf space or such a global submersion, so this argument does not prove the stronger bound for every foliation.',r'This is the naturality-and-dimension motivation for the weaker universal \(2q\) bound. The normal-bundle interpretation agrees with \citet[section~4]{bott1976characteristic}.',refs=['bott1976characteristic'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
item(206,'P000329','remark','Planned analytic fundamental solutions and finite-dimensional branching',r'Beside Gabrielov and Bernstein, three goals are listed for constant-coefficient equations: analyticity outside finite codimension, continuation throughout complex \(\mathbf C^n\), and a finite-dimensional space of branches.',r'The passage is a program, not a fully specified theorem. It leaves unstated the class of differential operators, the distributional or holomorphic meaning of a fundamental solution, the exceptional set to be removed, and the paths along which continuation is allowed. The finite-dimensionality phrase may concern the span of branch germs; it does not literally mean that only finitely many branches occur.',r'The source and adjacent later Bernstein passages were compared with the general analytic-continuation/holonomic research line. No exact primary statement establishing all three assertions with the missing hypotheses was verified. Consequently no universal continuation theorem or new open problem is extracted from this list.',refs=[],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='medium',status='The intended precise theorem and its exact publication remain unidentified; the three phrases are retained as programmatic content.')
item(206,'P000330','construction','The quartic anharmonic-oscillator spectral problem',r'The differential equation is \(-y^{\prime\prime}+x^2y+\beta x^4y=\lambda y\), introduced under a cancelled speaker name.',r'The natural spectral interpretation is the one-dimensional Schrödinger operator \(H_\beta=-d^2/dx^2+x^2+\beta x^4\) on \(L^2(\mathbf R)\), initially for real \(\beta>0\), with the square-integrable/decaying boundary conditions. Its discrete eigenvalues may then be studied by analytic continuation in complex \(\beta\); the continuation requires specifying sheets and boundary sectors.',r'The model agrees after rescaling with \citet[abstract]{benderwu1969anharmonic}, published 25 August 1969, and with the title and scope of \citet{simon1970coupling}, published 1970. The boundary conditions are explicit in Bender--Wu, but omitted in the notebook.',refs=['benderwu1969anharmonic','simon1970coupling'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',verification='publisher metadata checked',reconstruction='The Hilbert-space and decay conditions are supplied from the identified spectral model; the cancelled name does not establish a seminar speaker.',speaker='Unattributed adjacent remark')
item(206,'P000330','claim','Accumulating branch points and the three-sheeted description',r'A provisionally countable number of second-order branch points is said to tend, as \(\beta\to0\), to a third-order branch point; the work is called semi-physical.',r'The identifiable content is the Bender--Wu analytic continuation of quartic-oscillator levels, with branch singularities accumulating at zero. Zero is not an isolated ordinary algebraic branch point. A three-sheeted global description must be distinguished from a local Puiseux singularity of order three, and the notebook\'s unrestricted assertion that every relevant branch point is quadratic is not verified by the retrieved abstract.',r'\citet[abstract]{benderwu1969anharmonic} explicitly gives an infinite accumulation of branch points and uses WKB methods. \citet{simon1970coupling} is the rigorous 1970 follow-up; its publisher search abstract describes global three-sheeted monodromy, but the full paper was not read in this run. Thus the topic is securely matched, while the exact compressed branch-order statement has only a partial match.',refs=['benderwu1969anharmonic','simon1970coupling'],literal='not determinable',match='partial',verification='indirect',confidence='medium',development='annotated',status='The nonisolated singularity and continuation problem were already studied before the seminar. The literal branch-order slogan is not promoted to an exactly verified theorem.',speaker='Unattributed adjacent remark')
problem('GP-1970-26','Homotoping a distribution to a foliation',r'Is every smooth constant-rank subbundle of a tangent bundle homotopic through subbundles to the tangent distribution of a nonsingular foliation?',['1970-206.1','1970-206.2'],r'Negative in general, as reported from Bott\'s pre-seminar integrability obstruction. Normal Pontryagin-ring classes in degrees above twice the codimension obstruct such a homotopy. The compressed CP² example is not securely identified and is not needed to state the obstruction.',['bott1970integrability','bott1976characteristic'])
corr('P000329','Bundle and degree in the Bott bound',r'\(\operatorname{Pontr}_i^k(\ldots)=0\) for \(k>2\dim\) of the bundle.',r'The normal bundle \(TM/E\), real rank \(q\), has Pontryagin-ring classes zero in total cohomological degree greater than \(2q\).',r'The displayed submersion sequence identifies the intended quotient bundle. The source leaves the degree convention compressed; Bott\'s primary later exposition identifies the normal Pontryagin classes.',typ='ambiguity')
corr('P000330','Anharmonic-oscillator branch-order slogan','Second-order branch points tend to a third-order branch point at zero.','Distinguish infinitely accumulating singularities at a nonisolated origin from a global three-sheeted continuation; do not infer an isolated cubic Puiseux point.','Bender--Wu explicitly says the origin is nonisolated. Simon\'s rigorous follow-up is securely identified bibliographically but was not read in full.',typ='ambiguity')

talk(207,'1971-02-08','D. A. Kazhdan','P000329-P000331','Catalogued Kazhdan entry without a securely attributable surviving block',r'The catalog names Kazhdan on 8 February, but these pages do not preserve an independent, securely readable Kazhdan lecture block. P000330 begins with a cancelled name above the quartic oscillator; P000331 has a different cancelled Ka\ldots dan-like name inside the continuation of Fedosov\'s Bott discussion. Neither cancellation licenses assigning a new mathematical theorem to this catalog record. The entry is retained with its attribution uncertainty and no invented mathematical items.',kind='other')
corr('P000329-P000331','Record 207 attribution','Catalogued Kazhdan lecture dated 8 February over overlapping pages.','Retain the catalog entry, but do not assign the oscillator or the cancelled intervention to it without additional evidence.','Scans P000330 and P000331 checked. The first name is cancelled and ambiguous; the second is within the continuing Bott/Fedosov block. No independent positive heading establishes the catalog boundary.',typ='provenance')

talk(208,'1971-02-08','B. V. Fedosov','P000330-P000331','Bott’s residue formula for holomorphic vector fields',r'The Fedosov block starts at the clearly marked name below the oscillator on P000330. It gives the zero linearization and the residue expression for Chern numbers, then continues at the top of P000331 with its infinitesimal-Lefschetz interpretation and a real-analogue remark. It ends at the separator before the next-week announcement and the 15 February heading. The manuscript\'s tentative 1965/66 date is corrected by the explicit 1967 bibliography in record 210.')
item(208,'P000330','construction','Linearization at an isolated holomorphic zero',r'\(\Theta(X)Y=[X,Y]\); at a zero \(p\), this depends only on \(Y_p\), and nondegeneracy means \(\Theta_p\) is invertible.',r'If \(X\) is a holomorphic vector field and \(X(p)=0\), the map \(\Theta_p:T_pM\to T_pM\), \(Y_p\mapsto[X,Y]_p\), is well defined. With the usual Lie bracket it equals \(-dX_p\). A nondegenerate zero has \(\det dX_p\ne0\), and is therefore isolated. Reversing the sign convention does not change the degree-\(m\) residue quotient.',r'The Lie-derivative endomorphism is the construction used in \citet[section~3.4]{bruzzorubtsov2012localization}, which recovers \citet{bott1967vector}. The notebook has the right fibrewise dependence at a zero.',refs=['bott1967vector','bruzzorubtsov2012localization'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(208,'P000330','theorem','The isolated-zero Bott formula computes Chern numbers',r'For compact complex \(M\), a holomorphic field with finitely many nondegenerate zeros computes all Chern classes; the displayed formula is \(\overline\psi=\sum_p\psi(\Theta_p)/\det\Theta_p\).',r'Let \(M\) be a compact complex manifold of complex dimension \(m\), and \(X\) a holomorphic vector field with only isolated nondegenerate zeros. For a symmetric homogeneous polynomial \(\psi\) of degree \(m\) in the Chern roots, let \(\Psi(c_1,\ldots,c_m)\) be its expression in elementary symmetric functions. Then \[\int_M\Psi(c_1(TM),\ldots,c_m(TM))=\sum_{X(p)=0}\frac{\psi(\Theta_p)}{\det\Theta_p}.\] This computes characteristic numbers, not all cohomology classes themselves. Symmetry of \(\psi\) is required to make the expression independent of ordering of eigenvalues.',r'This is the isolated-zero result of \citet{bott1967vector}; the same-year extension is \citet{bott1967residue}. Publication metadata and the original abstracts were opened. The exact formula was read in \citet[section~3.4]{bruzzorubtsov2012localization}, rather than inferred solely from a title.',refs=['bott1967vector','bott1967residue','bruzzorubtsov2012localization'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated',status='Established before the seminar. Compactness, holomorphicity, symmetric polynomial degree, and isolated nondegenerate zeros delimit this displayed formula.')
item(208,'P000330','theorem','Lower-degree residue sums vanish',r'The parenthesis says that if the polynomial degree is less than \(m\), the sum is zero.',r'With the same compact holomorphic setup and a symmetric homogeneous polynomial \(\psi\) of degree \(d<m\), \(\sum_p\psi(\Theta_p)/\det\Theta_p=0\). This is a lower-degree localization identity. No corresponding vanishing for all degrees \(d>m\) is asserted.',r'The vanishing is the lower-degree part of Bott localization; \citet[Theorem~3.2 and section~3.4]{bruzzorubtsov2012localization} supplies the general localization mechanism, and its equation~(15) displays the constant-polynomial instance. The source correctly states only the lower-degree case.',refs=['bott1967vector','bruzzorubtsov2012localization'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='exact',verification='read the result')
item(208,'P000331','remark','The infinitesimal fixed-point interpretation',r'The formula is called an infinitesimal analogue of the Atiyah--Bott theorem because exponentiating the field gives a transformation of the manifold.',r'A holomorphic vector field generates a local holomorphic flow. Linearizing the fixed-point formulas for the flow at its stationary points relates the infinitesimal data \(dX_p\) to the characteristic-number residues. The notebook gives this conceptual relationship, not a complete limiting argument or a new fixed-point theorem.',r'The named fixed-point theory is \citet{atiyahbott1967lefschetz}; the original journal metadata was opened. Bott\'s residue formula is independently verified through \citet[section~3.4]{bruzzorubtsov2012localization}. The relationship is reported at the level of the notebook\'s remark and is not presented as a proof that was read in the original articles.',refs=['atiyahbott1967lefschetz','bott1967vector','bruzzorubtsov2012localization'],literal='correct',match='partial',verification='indirect')
item(208,'P000331','remark','The real analogue and the comparison with Morse inequalities',r'A real analogue is mentioned, with exact numerical values rather than estimates as in Morse theory; the preceding name is cancelled.',r'Bott-type characteristic-number formulas also have a real metric-preserving vector-field setting. The required real hypotheses are additional structure, not a statement about arbitrary smooth vector fields determining all characteristic numbers. The comparison with Morse theory concerns the exact sums in localization formulas; it does not replace all Morse inequalities by equalities or compute every Betti number.',r'\citet[introduction]{bruzzorubtsov2012localization} explicitly distinguishes the holomorphic and Riemannian-metric-preserving cases of \citet{bott1967vector}. The real case is treated more concretely in the subsequent Fedosov record 212; this early remark is retained with a partial publication match and no attribution to a cancelled speaker.',refs=['bott1967vector','bruzzorubtsov2012localization'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',verification='read the result',confidence='medium')
corr('P000330','Bott bibliography date','1965, 66; Michigan.','Bott, Vector fields and characteristic numbers, Michigan Math. J. 14(2) (1967), 231–244.','The complete citation on P000331 and the opened author bibliography agree on 1967. Earlier circulation was not established by this search.',typ='provenance')
corr('P000330','Chern classes versus numbers','Using the field one can compute all Chern classes.','The displayed isolated-zero formula computes all Chern numbers.','The next lines define the corresponding Chern number by integration. The mathematical formula and its primary modern verification concern characteristic numbers.')
corr('P000330','Polynomial hypothesis',r'\(\psi(X_1,\ldots,X_m)\) is homogeneous of degree \(m\).',r'Require \(\psi\) symmetric, equivalently use an invariant polynomial of matrices; its degree in Chern roots is \(m\).','The source subsequently expresses the polynomial through elementary symmetric functions, which requires symmetry. The degree convention differs from weighted Chern-class degree only by that expression.')

